WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.951
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4535.

2
00:00:00.951 --> 00:00:01.902
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.902 --> 00:00:03.488
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Today is October thirty-first, 2015.

4
00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:14.585
We're at the Rigmaiden Recreational Center on the Old Spanish Trail and I'm here . . . I'm Chelsea Arseneault with the [LSU Libraries] T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History and I'm here today with Mr. Jackson.

5
00:00:14.585 --> 00:00:14.902
<v ROGER CLAY JACKSON>Hi.

6
00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:15.854
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Rogers Jackson.

7
00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:16.171
<v JACKSON>Right.

8
00:00:16.171 --> 00:00:17.122
Roger Clay Jackson.

9
00:00:17.122 --> 00:00:17.439
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

10
00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:22.512
We're here today to talk to him about his memories about growing up in Mossville, Louisiana,

11
00:00:22.512 --> 00:00:28.854
as a part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

12
00:00:28.854 --> 00:00:33.927
We want to thank you for taking the time to share your story with us today.

13
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:39.000
Now, the first thing I want to ask is did you grow up in Mossville?

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.413
<v JACKSON>Yes.

15
00:00:39.413 --> 00:00:39.825
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

16
00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:44.365
Just wanted to make sure before I started asking any questions.

17
00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:48.492
So what date . . . So when and where were you born?

18
00:00:48.492 --> 00:00:54.270
<v JACKSON>I was born in . . . I was born in Shreveport, Louisiana, on June nineteenth, 1947.

19
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:54.683
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

20
00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:59.222
And what were your parents’ names . . . or are your parents’ names?

21
00:00:59.222 --> 00:01:01.286
<v JACKSON>Allen Jackson and Beatrice Jackson.

22
00:01:01.286 --> 00:01:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And what do they do for a living?

23
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.639
<v JACKSON>Now?

24
00:01:05.639 --> 00:01:11.393
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they do when you were growing up?

25
00:01:11.393 --> 00:01:12.033
<v JACKSON>Oh.

26
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:20.984
My father worked on the railroad and my mother did housekeeping for different people.

27
00:01:20.984 --> 00:01:21.623
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

28
00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:26.098
How . . . When did they move to Mossville?

29
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:37.607
<v JACKSON>I guess probably . . . I was ten . . . now I’m fifty-seven . . . I was ten . . . so probably about nineteen . . . Let’s see.

30
00:01:37.607 --> 00:01:38.885
About 1950.

31
00:01:38.885 --> 00:01:40.803
Yes, about 1950.

32
00:01:40.803 --> 00:01:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What brought them here?

33
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.217
<v JACKSON>I can't really say.

34
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:48.988
I was a small kid.

35
00:01:48.988 --> 00:01:52.313
I just came with my parents.

36
00:01:52.313 --> 00:01:56.193
I don't know why they moved here.

37
00:01:56.193 --> 00:02:00.072
<v ARSENEAULT>They never talked about it or anything?

38
00:02:00.072 --> 00:02:03.952
<v JACKSON>No. Probably because of economic reasons probably.

39
00:02:03.952 --> 00:02:07.831
That's the best I can think of.

40
00:02:07.831 --> 00:02:15.036
They never . . . If they did, I was too small to really know why.

41
00:02:15.036 --> 00:02:20.024
Then my mother died when I was ten so . . .

42
00:02:20.024 --> 00:02:23.904
<v ARSENEAULT>And what was your mother's maiden name?

43
00:02:23.904 --> 00:02:24.458
<v JACKSON>Parsons[?].

44
00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:25.566
Beatrice Parsons.

45
00:02:25.566 --> 00:02:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And how many children did they have?

46
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.238
<v JACKSON>There were five of us—four boys and we had a sister.

47
00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:37.619
<v ARSENEAULT>And what number are you?

48
00:02:37.619 --> 00:02:39.048
<v JACKSON>I'm the baby.

49
00:02:39.048 --> 00:02:40.476
<v ARSENEAULT>You're the youngest?

50
00:02:40.476 --> 00:02:41.905
<v JACKSON>The last one.

51
00:02:41.905 --> 00:02:42.381
Yes.

52
00:02:42.381 --> 00:02:44.286
I made it here.

53
00:02:44.286 --> 00:02:46.667
<v ARSENEAULT>And what about your grandparents?

54
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:50.000
What do you know about them?

55
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.990
<v JACKSON>After my mother passed my grandmother raised me, raised me and my sisters and brothers.

56
00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:02.583
And my grandfather, he was there, but I mean they were together.

57
00:03:02.583 --> 00:03:14.233
But I say my grandmother because she had a greater influence on my life than anybody else growing . . . during the time we was growing up.

58
00:03:14.233 --> 00:03:17.961
<v ARSENEAULT>And what was their . . . what were their names?

59
00:03:17.961 --> 00:03:20.291
<v JACKSON>George Parsons and Florence Parsons.

60
00:03:20.291 --> 00:03:23.553
<v ARSENEAULT>And when did they come to Mossville?

61
00:03:23.553 --> 00:03:25.883
<v JACKSON>Oh, I couldn't exactly say.

62
00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:31.010
They were here when we came so I really don't know.

63
00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.738
But they were here before we moved here.

64
00:03:34.738 --> 00:03:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And that was your mom's parents?

65
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:38.681
<v JACKSON>Yes.

66
00:03:38.681 --> 00:03:42.087
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your dad's parents?

67
00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:54.348
<v JACKSON>As far as I know, they were living . . . When we moved they were still living in Shreveport, Louisiana.

68
00:03:54.348 --> 00:03:59.797
Some of his family lived in Dallas, Texas.

69
00:03:59.797 --> 00:04:01.841
Still live there.

70
00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:08.652
But my father passed maybe in 1981 . . . ’82 in California.

71
00:04:08.652 --> 00:04:14.101
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did your parents live in Mossville?

72
00:04:14.101 --> 00:04:25.681
<v JACKSON>Oh, my mother passed in ’57, and I guess maybe seven to ten years living in Mossville.

73
00:04:25.681 --> 00:04:33.174
My father passed . . . No, he didn't pass right after my mother.

74
00:04:33.174 --> 00:04:46.116
But after my mother passed he got in some trouble and went to the penitentiary for about three years.

75
00:04:46.116 --> 00:04:53.609
After that he moved to Dallas, Texas when he got out.

76
00:04:53.609 --> 00:04:58.377
So from Dallas he moved to California.

77
00:04:58.377 --> 00:05:03.145
He passed in California around 1981 . . . ‘82.

78
00:05:03.145 --> 00:05:05.188
Not exactly sure.

79
00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:09.275
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was y'alls house in Mossville?

80
00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:12.000
Where was it?

81
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.805
<v JACKSON>Located down . . . Wait I may be giving you the wrong information.

82
00:05:16.805 --> 00:05:23.795
I grew up . . . I went to school here, but I grew up really in Westlake [Louisiana].

83
00:05:23.795 --> 00:05:24.232
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

84
00:05:24.232 --> 00:05:33.842
See we're trying to figure out where Mossville started and where it ended, and I know the boundary between Westlake and Mossville.

85
00:05:33.842 --> 00:05:36.463
<v JACKSON>It's a whole two different things.

86
00:05:36.463 --> 00:05:38.647
I grew up in Westlake.

87
00:05:38.647 --> 00:05:44.763
I went to high school and to elementary and junior high school in Mossville.

88
00:05:44.763 --> 00:05:45.200
Yes.

89
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.132
And my parents, we really didn't move to Mossville.

90
00:05:49.132 --> 00:05:50.879
We moved to Westlake.

91
00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:52.626
That's what it was.

92
00:05:52.626 --> 00:05:59.616
Yeah, and where my parents lived in Westlake was on Simpson Street and . . . 1310 Simpson Street.

93
00:05:59.616 --> 00:06:01.800
I remember our address there.

94
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.353
Across the street from the Catholic Church, Saint John Bosco, which is still there now.

95
00:06:08.353 --> 00:06:13.158
And my grandmother's house is still there and my parents’ house.

96
00:06:13.158 --> 00:06:21.458
We lived with my grandmother and then they had a little house on the side on the same property.

97
00:06:21.458 --> 00:06:25.389
And my cousin lives now in my grandmother's house.

98
00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:28.011
It’s still standing there in Westlake.

99
00:06:28.011 --> 00:06:31.068
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that where y'all went to church?

100
00:06:31.068 --> 00:06:31.505
<v JACKSON>Pardon?

101
00:06:31.505 --> 00:06:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that where y'all went to church?

102
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.944
<v JACKSON>At Saint John Bosco?

103
00:06:36.944 --> 00:06:38.888
No, we were Baptist.

104
00:06:38.888 --> 00:06:41.318
It was the Catholic Church.

105
00:06:41.318 --> 00:06:46.178
It was built during the time we was growing up.

106
00:06:46.178 --> 00:06:50.551
I went to church at Good Hope Baptist Church.

107
00:06:50.551 --> 00:07:00.271
It was down maybe about half a mile from the Catholic Church but on the same street, on Sampson Street.

108
00:07:00.271 --> 00:07:04.645
<v ARSENEAULT>And what did your house look like growing up?

109
00:07:04.645 --> 00:07:09.019
<v JACKSON>What do you mean what did it look like?

110
00:07:09.019 --> 00:07:10.477
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember?

111
00:07:10.477 --> 00:07:12.421
Can you describe it?

112
00:07:12.421 --> 00:07:13.879
<v JACKSON>It was little.

113
00:07:13.879 --> 00:07:14.364
Plain.

114
00:07:14.364 --> 00:07:16.794
It wasn't a brick house.

115
00:07:16.794 --> 00:07:22.140
It was a wood frame house with asbestos siding on it.

116
00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, because they used that back in the day.

117
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.449
<v JACKSON>It was about three bedrooms, kitchen, hallway, bathroom.

118
00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:33.899
It was an okay house, for that time.

119
00:07:33.899 --> 00:07:38.642
My grandmother kept her house real clean, and my mother too.

120
00:07:38.642 --> 00:07:40.797
My grandmother insisted on it.

121
00:07:40.797 --> 00:07:42.953
She was a true soldier.

122
00:07:42.953 --> 00:07:45.971
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things would she do?

123
00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:46.834
Just like . . .

124
00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:54.164
<v JACKSON>Oh this is after my mother passed and stuff and we were staying directly with my grandmother.

125
00:07:54.164 --> 00:08:04.081
She taught us how to wash clothes, how to sort clothes out, how to iron clothes, how to cook, how to clean up.

126
00:08:04.081 --> 00:08:08.823
You did it, because if you didn't, you were in trouble.

127
00:08:08.823 --> 00:08:18.309
It didn't take a whole lot of persuading from my grandmother to get you to do what you were supposed to do.

128
00:08:18.309 --> 00:08:25.208
Me and my brother and my sister, we did whatever my grandmother told us to do,

129
00:08:25.208 --> 00:08:32.969
and then you had your free time but you couldn’t go out and . . . I loved to play baseball.

130
00:08:32.969 --> 00:08:34.694
My brothers did too.

131
00:08:34.694 --> 00:08:38.574
But you had to do all your work first.

132
00:08:38.574 --> 00:08:42.023
You had to do your ironing and stuff.

133
00:08:42.023 --> 00:08:48.060
My grandmother didn't believe in getting up and ironing when it was school time.

134
00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.078
You ironed all your clothes and stuff.

135
00:08:51.078 --> 00:08:56.252
You got up early Saturday and you'd wash your jeans and everything,

136
00:08:56.252 --> 00:09:07.031
and dry them out and by the time you got a chance to get away, it would be maybe three or four in the evening.

137
00:09:07.031 --> 00:09:13.068
You had to do all your washing and ironing and stuff on one day.

138
00:09:13.068 --> 00:09:15.655
That was how it was done.

139
00:09:15.655 --> 00:09:18.242
My grandmother was a great cook.

140
00:09:18.242 --> 00:09:26.434
We had a lot of . . . We weren't rich or anything, but we always had a lot of good food.

141
00:09:26.434 --> 00:09:29.452
My grandmother cooked enough all the time.

142
00:09:29.452 --> 00:09:38.075
Her only thing about eating was if you got seconds or whatever, you could eat as much as you want,

143
00:09:38.075 --> 00:09:44.543
but as long . . . If you take it . . . if you ask for it, you eat it.

144
00:09:44.543 --> 00:09:52.304
Not eat some of it and throw it away because she didn't believe in about throwing food away.

145
00:09:52.304 --> 00:09:54.460
We had some great times.

146
00:09:54.460 --> 00:10:00.496
My grandmother used to have the Baptist preacher come and eat at our house,

147
00:10:00.496 --> 00:10:09.551
so the Baptist preacher know where the good food is and he would come and eat on Sundays at our house.

148
00:10:09.551 --> 00:10:13.000
My grandmother was the best cook around.

149
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.126
I learned a lot from her.

150
00:10:15.126 --> 00:10:19.024
Not only to cook, but like to preserve figs and pears,

151
00:10:19.024 --> 00:10:25.047
and how to make peach cobblers, blackberry cobblers, and biscuits, and yeast rolls, and all of that.

152
00:10:25.047 --> 00:10:26.819
Still can do it now.

153
00:10:26.819 --> 00:10:34.614
I mean, I learned from . . . I brought some chili today that was real great I made from just learning watching my grandmother.

154
00:10:34.614 --> 00:10:40.638
I used to be around a lot in the kitchen and she'd have me get this thing,

155
00:10:40.638 --> 00:10:43.118
get this . . . mixing up things and stuff.

156
00:10:43.118 --> 00:10:47.724
I was always asking questions and stuff about why she was doing this.

157
00:10:47.724 --> 00:10:49.142
Why you doing it?

158
00:10:49.142 --> 00:10:51.268
Why you put this in it?

159
00:10:51.268 --> 00:10:55.520
That's the way I learned to cook with my grandmother growing up.

160
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she have a famous recipe?

161
00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.321
<v JACKSON>A famous one?

162
00:10:59.321 --> 00:11:03.726
No. Look like everything was . . . My grandmother could cook everything.

163
00:11:03.726 --> 00:11:10.774
German . . . That's the very first German chocolate cake I ever ate was one my grandmother made.

164
00:11:10.774 --> 00:11:14.298
My grandmother used to make real coconut cakes.

165
00:11:14.298 --> 00:11:20.905
I mean, when you have to break the coconut and grind it and all this.

166
00:11:20.905 --> 00:11:22.226
The real coconut.

167
00:11:22.226 --> 00:11:24.429
<v ARSENEAULT>No one does that anymore.

168
00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:26.631
<v JACKSON>Not from in the store.

169
00:11:26.631 --> 00:11:28.833
You used the real coconut.

170
00:11:28.833 --> 00:11:30.595
Yes, there it is.

171
00:11:30.595 --> 00:11:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite food she would make?

172
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.391
<v JACKSON>No, because everything seemed to be good.

173
00:11:38.391 --> 00:11:38.875
Everything!

174
00:11:38.875 --> 00:11:42.266
It was hard to have a favorite.

175
00:11:42.266 --> 00:11:43.719
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s Mr. Leroy.

176
00:11:43.719 --> 00:11:45.172
<v JACKSON>Oh, that's Blunt.

177
00:11:45.172 --> 00:11:47.109
Yes, you know him?

178
00:11:47.109 --> 00:11:50.016
<v ARSENEAULT>We're trying to interview his mom.

179
00:11:50.016 --> 00:11:51.469
<v JACKSON>You know him?

180
00:11:51.469 --> 00:11:51.953
Okay.

181
00:11:51.953 --> 00:11:52.922
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Leroy.

182
00:11:52.922 --> 00:11:53.406
<v JACKSON>Yes.

183
00:11:53.406 --> 00:11:54.375
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, indeed.

184
00:11:54.375 --> 00:11:55.344
Ms. Vera.

185
00:11:55.344 --> 00:11:57.766
<v JACKSON>He in his camouflage stuff.

186
00:11:57.766 --> 00:11:58.734
<v ARSENEAULT>Looking good.

187
00:11:58.734 --> 00:12:01.641
<v JACKSON>Look like . . . Yes, a great dude.

188
00:12:01.641 --> 00:12:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So did y'all have a garden growing up?

189
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:06.394
<v JACKSON>Yes.

190
00:12:06.394 --> 00:12:09.156
My grandfather was like a professional farmer.

191
00:12:09.156 --> 00:12:14.284
He could . . . He would . . . My grandmother could cook and he could grow everything.

192
00:12:14.284 --> 00:12:15.862
<v ARSENEAULT>That's the perfect couple.

193
00:12:15.862 --> 00:12:16.257
<v JACKSON>Yes.

194
00:12:16.257 --> 00:12:17.835
He could grow everything.

195
00:12:17.835 --> 00:12:21.385
I'm talking about like peanuts and strawberries and sugarcane.

196
00:12:21.385 --> 00:12:29.275
Anything my grandfather . . . you could think of that, within reason, people ate like greens, and cabbage, and squash, okra anything.

197
00:12:29.275 --> 00:12:32.037
We had a garden, a big garden,

198
00:12:32.037 --> 00:12:45.055
and a well that you could put a watermelon in a bucket and sit it down into the spring water when it chills and gets cold you pull it up and cut it.

199
00:12:45.055 --> 00:12:45.844
That's . . . Yes.

200
00:12:45.844 --> 00:12:49.394
You don't know anything about that I don't think.

201
00:12:49.394 --> 00:12:51.761
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds like the good old days.

202
00:12:51.761 --> 00:12:52.156
<v JACKSON>Yes.

203
00:12:52.156 --> 00:13:03.596
Put a watermelon in the bucket let it down into the water and sit it in there for about thirty minutes and the water was coming like from underground,

204
00:13:03.596 --> 00:13:07.936
and the well would always be . . . it had water in it.

205
00:13:07.936 --> 00:13:09.908
It was an underground spring.

206
00:13:09.908 --> 00:13:15.826
He'd drill down make a well and you just put the water melon in there.

207
00:13:15.826 --> 00:13:24.110
You could also put it in the refrigerator, but we thought it tasted better and stuff doing it in a well.

208
00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:31.211
We all . . . my brothers and I liked to put it down in the well and get it cold.

209
00:13:31.211 --> 00:13:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Cooler.

210
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:32.392
<v JACKSON>Yes.

211
00:13:32.392 --> 00:13:34.354
It seemed to be anyway.

212
00:13:34.354 --> 00:13:37.492
See we always thought it tastes better. [laughs]

213
00:13:37.492 --> 00:13:39.846
<v ARSENEAULT>There may be something to it.

214
00:13:39.846 --> 00:13:41.415
<v JACKSON>Yes, the natural thing.

215
00:13:41.415 --> 00:13:41.807
Yes.

216
00:13:41.807 --> 00:13:44.553
<v ARSENEAULT>So your grandfather was a professional farmer?

217
00:13:44.553 --> 00:13:46.514
<v JACKSON>That's what I call him.

218
00:13:46.514 --> 00:13:47.299
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he . . .

219
00:13:47.299 --> 00:13:53.576
<v JACKSON>But he didn't sell stuff to feed . . . He gave a lot of stuff away and stuff.

220
00:13:53.576 --> 00:13:58.283
I call him a professional farmer because he could do it all.

221
00:13:58.283 --> 00:13:58.675
Yes.

222
00:13:58.675 --> 00:14:01.029
He knew how to do everything.

223
00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:04.559
I mean, he knew how to make things grow.

224
00:14:04.559 --> 00:14:10.836
In the watching plant things it was like he had some rows of . . . in the garden.

225
00:14:10.836 --> 00:14:14.367
You know what a garden row looks like, right?

226
00:14:14.367 --> 00:14:19.859
He would like have like seeds and things too, like mustard seeds or whatever.

227
00:14:19.859 --> 00:14:25.350
In the ground, in the row, he would put his thing in like this,

228
00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:32.412
like stick it down in here, and drop a little seed or whatever and just cover it up.

229
00:14:32.412 --> 00:14:39.080
Because he would use his finger, and go all the way down, and that's how he planted.

230
00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:46.926
In planting, he said not to plant the same crop the next year where you had planted a crop stuff.

231
00:14:46.926 --> 00:14:56.341
If he planted corn this year in this spot, in this area, in these rows or whatever, change it around and plant something different.

232
00:14:56.341 --> 00:14:59.479
Don't plant the same thing there next year.

233
00:14:59.479 --> 00:15:04.579
I don't know what was . . . Well the purpose was that it wouldn't grow.

234
00:15:04.579 --> 00:15:06.148
It wouldn't grow good.

235
00:15:06.148 --> 00:15:11.640
It wouldn't grow if you put the same thing right there the next year.

236
00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.209
It wouldn't grow good.

237
00:15:13.209 --> 00:15:19.486
I mean, it would grow but it would be a big harvest like it was before.

238
00:15:19.486 --> 00:15:23.408
So it was better to change whatever you grew here.

239
00:15:23.408 --> 00:15:28.508
If you grew corn here, put some mustard greens here the next year.

240
00:15:28.508 --> 00:15:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he plant by the moon or did he plant by the almanac?

241
00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.958
<v JACKSON>[laughs] I couldn't tell you.

242
00:15:35.958 --> 00:15:39.090
Whatever it was the moon or the almanac.

243
00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:48.096
All I know is my grandfather planted things each spring, and in winter, or whatever time of year it was to plant it.

244
00:15:48.096 --> 00:15:55.928
I don't know if he was looking at the moon or reading an almanac or what, but it would grow.

245
00:15:55.928 --> 00:15:58.277
<v ARSENEAULT>Whatever he was doing it worked.

246
00:15:58.277 --> 00:15:58.669
<v JACKSON>Yes.

247
00:15:58.669 --> 00:15:59.452
It worked.

248
00:15:59.452 --> 00:16:02.193
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have chores on the farm?

249
00:16:02.193 --> 00:16:04.151
<v JACKSON>We wasn't on a farm.

250
00:16:04.151 --> 00:16:06.500
We wasn't on a farm, lady.

251
00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:07.675
No, not really.

252
00:16:07.675 --> 00:16:24.120
I mean we kind of came to . . . Well you could say harvested but it wasn't . . . It was a nice sized garden but we helped out my grandfather when he needed like to cut the okra or whatever when the stuff was ripe.

253
00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:28.036
Yes, we pitched in and helped them . . . got it in.

254
00:16:28.036 --> 00:16:33.518
Yes, my grandmother preserved a lot of food in jars and things like jelly.

255
00:16:33.518 --> 00:16:35.867
You know what a mayhaw is?

256
00:16:35.867 --> 00:16:39.000
Or have you ever heard of them?

257
00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:40.286
<v ARSENEAULT>I've heard of mayhaw jelly.

258
00:16:40.286 --> 00:16:40.543
<v JACKSON>Yes.

259
00:16:40.543 --> 00:16:42.600
<v ARSENEAULT>But I don't know what a mayhaw is.

260
00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:43.371
<v JACKSON>Yes, mayhaw jelly.

261
00:16:43.371 --> 00:16:45.171
You make the jelly from the mayhaw.

262
00:16:45.171 --> 00:16:46.200
<v ARSENEAULT>What is a mayhaw?

263
00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.000
<v JACKSON>You remember mayhaw jelly then right?

264
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:52.750
<v ARSENEAULT>I've heard of the jelly, but I don't know what a mayhaw is.

265
00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.942
<v JACKSON>A mayhaw is a wild plum.

266
00:16:54.942 --> 00:16:55.308
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

267
00:16:55.308 --> 00:16:55.673
<v JACKSON>Yes.

268
00:16:55.673 --> 00:16:58.231
It looks like a little red apple.

269
00:16:58.231 --> 00:17:02.981
I mean, like it's more . . . You know what the . . . What you call it?

270
00:17:02.981 --> 00:17:04.077
A maraschino cherry?

271
00:17:04.077 --> 00:17:04.442
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

272
00:17:04.442 --> 00:17:07.000
<v JACKSON>The cherry with the long stem.

273
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:07.645
<v ARSENEAULT>The maraschino.

274
00:17:07.645 --> 00:17:07.968
<v JACKSON>Yes.

275
00:17:07.968 --> 00:17:10.226
Well this is what it looked like.

276
00:17:10.226 --> 00:17:13.452
But it's not red it's more of an orange color.

277
00:17:13.452 --> 00:17:13.774
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

278
00:17:13.774 --> 00:17:14.742
<v JACKSON>Not really red.

279
00:17:14.742 --> 00:17:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it's not really plum color?

280
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:17.337
<v JACKSON>Pardon?

281
00:17:17.337 --> 00:17:22.048
<v ARSENEAULT>It's not the color of a plum, but it's the size of a plum?

282
00:17:22.048 --> 00:17:25.413
<v JACKSON>No not the size of . . . It's like the small apple.

283
00:17:25.413 --> 00:17:26.423
I mean like . . .

284
00:17:26.423 --> 00:17:29.452
<v ARSENEAULT>It's the size of a cherry and it's yellow.

285
00:17:29.452 --> 00:17:31.135
<v JACKSON>No, it's not really yellow.

286
00:17:31.135 --> 00:17:31.808
It's orange.

287
00:17:31.808 --> 00:17:33.490
It's got an orange color.

288
00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:38.538
It may have a little yellowish color to it too, but it's a wild plum.

289
00:17:38.538 --> 00:17:41.904
It's tart, but it makes some of the best jelly.

290
00:17:41.904 --> 00:17:45.606
Have you ever eaten any, or you just heard of it?

291
00:17:45.606 --> 00:17:47.288
<v ARSENEAULT>I've just heard of it.

292
00:17:47.288 --> 00:17:48.971
<v JACKSON>Yes it's a great jelly.

293
00:17:48.971 --> 00:17:49.644
<v ARSENEAULT>It's good?

294
00:17:49.644 --> 00:17:52.000
So how would she make it?

295
00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:01.038
<v JACKSON>Like most jellies you have to get the fruit, wash it, and then if you going to preserve it and can it,

296
00:18:01.038 --> 00:18:05.968
putting it in a jar, you have to wash the jars out.

297
00:18:05.968 --> 00:18:08.022
That's what you do first.

298
00:18:08.022 --> 00:18:14.184
Get your jars ready, but you going to have to deal with them again though.

299
00:18:14.184 --> 00:18:16.238
But you get them ready.

300
00:18:16.238 --> 00:18:18.703
You wash them in hot water.

301
00:18:18.703 --> 00:18:28.151
Then you boil them in hot water, take it out clean . . . I mean, rinse them out, and then boil it in hot water.

302
00:18:28.151 --> 00:18:32.259
This is to keep bacteria, to sterilize it really good.

303
00:18:32.259 --> 00:18:36.778
But the cooking process, you wash off the fruit, the mayhaws.

304
00:18:36.778 --> 00:18:38.422
Put sugar in it.

305
00:18:38.422 --> 00:18:45.405
The amount of sugar you want, but then you got to taste it once you boil it.

306
00:18:45.405 --> 00:18:47.870
Same way with berries or whatever.

307
00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:52.389
You put sugar in it and water and you boil it.

308
00:18:52.389 --> 00:18:54.443
And then you strain it.

309
00:18:54.443 --> 00:19:02.249
But once you strain it, okay, you ready to put it into . . . back into a pot that's just liquid.

310
00:19:02.249 --> 00:19:06.357
You have to add what you call a sure gel.

311
00:19:06.357 --> 00:19:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is it?

312
00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:08.738
<v JACKSON>Sure gel.

313
00:19:08.738 --> 00:19:09.476
<v ARSENEAULT>Sheer gel?

314
00:19:09.476 --> 00:19:10.214
<v JACKSON>Sure, S-U-R-E.

315
00:19:10.214 --> 00:19:10.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure gel.

316
00:19:10.952 --> 00:19:11.690
<v JACKSON>Sure gel.

317
00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:12.059
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

318
00:19:12.059 --> 00:19:12.428
<v JACKSON>Yes.

319
00:19:12.428 --> 00:19:15.011
It's a mixture to make it gel.

320
00:19:15.011 --> 00:19:22.021
Like Jell-O does, but you boil it and you add your sugar in there too, then into your mixture.

321
00:19:22.021 --> 00:19:27.556
I said you put sugar when you first put the fruit together, but you don't.

322
00:19:27.556 --> 00:19:31.246
You boil the fruit first and then you strain it.

323
00:19:31.246 --> 00:19:41.947
Once you put it on the fire and stuff you pour your liquid in and strain so there's nothing but clear liquid . . . but from the plum and the water.

324
00:19:41.947 --> 00:19:45.636
Then you add your sugar, and then your sure gel,

325
00:19:45.636 --> 00:19:53.754
and you're stirring around and let it cook for however amount of time it says on the sure gel box or whatever.

326
00:19:53.754 --> 00:19:55.968
My grandmother never worried about that.

327
00:19:55.968 --> 00:20:03.348
She had done it so many times growing up, she knew when it was time, whatever, to take it off.

328
00:20:03.348 --> 00:20:11.096
Then you put it in jars and then it's going to . . . You pour it in a pint jar . . . the Mason jar.

329
00:20:11.096 --> 00:20:14.048
You heard of a Mason jar, haven’t you?

330
00:20:14.048 --> 00:20:14.417
Okay.

331
00:20:14.417 --> 00:20:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It's come back in fashion now.

332
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:17.354
<v JACKSON>Right.

333
00:20:17.354 --> 00:20:17.708
Yes.

334
00:20:17.708 --> 00:20:24.792
So you pour it in there, and to seal it up you have to have a ring and a lid.

335
00:20:24.792 --> 00:20:25.854
<v ARSENEAULT>I've seen that.

336
00:20:25.854 --> 00:20:26.917
<v JACKSON>You know that?

337
00:20:26.917 --> 00:20:29.042
Then you put that on there.

338
00:20:29.042 --> 00:20:35.771
Then you put them back into some hot water in a pot, or whatever big enough, to cover them.

339
00:20:35.771 --> 00:20:38.250
You pour the water over the top.

340
00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:39.667
Heat your water again.

341
00:20:39.667 --> 00:20:40.375
Be boiling.

342
00:20:40.375 --> 00:20:43.208
<v ARSENEAULT>So you boil it . . . you boil the cans,

343
00:20:43.208 --> 00:20:48.875
then you boil the jelly, and then you boil it when it's back in the can?

344
00:20:48.875 --> 00:20:51.000
<v JACKSON>Yes, back into the jar.

345
00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:51.366
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

346
00:20:51.366 --> 00:20:56.494
<v JACKSON>You boil it again once you put the seal and the ring on it.

347
00:20:56.494 --> 00:21:01.988
That is for the purpose of . . . the ring . . . no, the seal has rubber on it.

348
00:21:01.988 --> 00:21:03.087
<v ARSENEAULT>And melts it?

349
00:21:03.087 --> 00:21:07.483
<v JACKSON>Yes, and when you screw the ring onto it, okay, it's tight.

350
00:21:07.483 --> 00:21:09.314
But it's not sealed good,

351
00:21:09.314 --> 00:21:26.163
so therefore you put it back into some . . . put it in some water and let the water boil and the heat from the water is going to melt that rubber some from inside and put the tighten on there and then that's how you seal it.

352
00:21:26.163 --> 00:21:29.459
Take it out once you know it’s boiled enough.

353
00:21:29.459 --> 00:21:33.488
You maybe leave it in there about fifteen to twenty minutes.

354
00:21:33.488 --> 00:21:36.785
Let it boil, and cut the fire or whatever.

355
00:21:36.785 --> 00:21:39.715
Take them out with some tongs or whatever.

356
00:21:39.715 --> 00:21:41.913
Set it down and you’re set.

357
00:21:41.913 --> 00:21:46.308
At that time it's still hot and liquid but it's going to,

358
00:21:46.308 --> 00:21:51.802
as it cools it's going to gel up to jelly because of the sure gel.

359
00:21:51.802 --> 00:21:53.267
So there you go.

360
00:21:53.267 --> 00:21:56.683
<c.p>ARSENEAULT Now I can make jelly.

361
00:21:56.683 --> 00:21:59.366
<v JACKSON>Yes, it's not that hard.

362
00:21:59.366 --> 00:22:02.585
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have livestock growing up?

363
00:22:02.585 --> 00:22:12.780
<v JACKSON>No. Oh, we had a . . . yes, a lot of chickens and turkeys and . . . You know what a guinea is?

364
00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:14.390
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a hen?

365
00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:14.927
Guineas?

366
00:22:14.927 --> 00:22:16.000
<v JACKSON>What?

367
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:17.769
<v ARSENEAULT>I don’t know, maybe not.

368
00:22:17.769 --> 00:22:19.892
It looks like a small chicken?

369
00:22:19.892 --> 00:22:20.246
<v JACKSON>Yes.

370
00:22:20.246 --> 00:22:22.723
It's shaped like a football kind of.

371
00:22:22.723 --> 00:22:23.431
It's gray.

372
00:22:23.431 --> 00:22:27.677
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s got a weird thing on its head like a little plume?

373
00:22:27.677 --> 00:22:28.738
<v JACKSON>Yes kind of.

374
00:22:28.738 --> 00:22:29.092
Yes.

375
00:22:29.092 --> 00:22:31.923
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I know what you're talking about.

376
00:22:31.923 --> 00:22:32.277
<v JACKSON>Yes.

377
00:22:32.277 --> 00:22:34.400
Look like a little small turkey.

378
00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:36.877
Small, yes, and shaped like a football.

379
00:22:36.877 --> 00:22:38.292
<v ARSENEAULT>I've actually eaten that.

380
00:22:38.292 --> 00:22:39.000
<v JACKSON>Pardon?

381
00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.627
<v ARSENEAULT>I've actually eaten that.

382
00:22:40.627 --> 00:22:41.033
<v JACKSON>Yes.

383
00:22:41.033 --> 00:22:43.473
They say it's good in gumbo.

384
00:22:43.473 --> 00:22:46.320
I've never eaten any guinea gumbo but . . .

385
00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:47.540
<v ARSENEAULT>That sounds good.

386
00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:51.607
<v JACKSON>. . . I hear it's . . . you eat it with your other ingredients.

387
00:22:51.607 --> 00:22:55.267
You could use parts of a guinea in gumbo.

388
00:22:55.267 --> 00:22:58.520
I'll tell you a story about a turkey.

389
00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:00.960
This is before my mother died.

390
00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:09.093
My grandfather had all, like I said, chickens and ducks and a lot of things like that . . . geese and turkeys.

391
00:23:09.093 --> 00:23:14.787
I would like to go and gather the eggs in a big chicken yard.

392
00:23:14.787 --> 00:23:18.853
It was fenced in, and it was kind of large.

393
00:23:18.853 --> 00:23:28.613
Anyway, it had what you call a chicken coop where the chickens would go at night, and roost and go to sleep or whatever.

394
00:23:28.613 --> 00:23:32.680
Anyway, I would like to gather eggs all the time.

395
00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.527
I would go and get the eggs.

396
00:23:35.527 --> 00:23:40.000
That was one of the things I liked to do.

397
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:49.156
So this time I went into the gate to go get the eggs, and I go into the chicken house where they roost there.

398
00:23:49.156 --> 00:23:55.260
I see kind of up on a little higher level, it was a lot of eggs.

399
00:23:55.260 --> 00:24:00.983
I saw a lot of eggs, and the turkey is sitting on the eggs there.

400
00:24:00.983 --> 00:24:02.509
It's not the chicken.

401
00:24:02.509 --> 00:24:04.416
I see all these eggs.

402
00:24:04.416 --> 00:24:05.942
I'm a little guy.

403
00:24:05.942 --> 00:24:06.705
That's exciting.

404
00:24:06.705 --> 00:24:14.335
I got my basket and I reach up to get the eggs, but this turkey hen is sitting on them.

405
00:24:14.335 --> 00:24:17.387
She’s going to have some little baby things.

406
00:24:17.387 --> 00:24:26.543
You get what's about to happen to me. [laughs] So, when I reach up to get the egg the turkey come off the nest.

407
00:24:26.543 --> 00:24:30.740
She’s mad because I’m fixing to take eggs from under her.

408
00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:32.647
I reached for the eggs.

409
00:24:32.647 --> 00:24:36.081
She come off the nest and peck at me.

410
00:24:36.081 --> 00:24:46.000
When she peck, I dropped the basket and turned around, but she in the air already to get me back here right in the back.

411
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.155
I take off.

412
00:24:47.155 --> 00:24:52.543
I don't go toward the gate, because I'm just running trying to get away.

413
00:24:52.543 --> 00:24:56.392
I run away from the gate where I came in,

414
00:24:56.392 --> 00:25:04.860
and the turkey is chasing me away from the gate down to like I'm going north, and she chasing me this way.

415
00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:09.094
The thing was like . . . The tag was right here . . . starts here.

416
00:25:09.094 --> 00:25:11.019
The gate is back here.

417
00:25:11.019 --> 00:25:18.332
I take off and go this way and she's behind me and flying to hit me in the back.

418
00:25:18.332 --> 00:25:19.487
I'm screaming murder.

419
00:25:19.487 --> 00:25:21.026
My mother runs outside.

420
00:25:21.026 --> 00:25:23.721
By this time I'm going west now.

421
00:25:23.721 --> 00:25:31.034
I’m running down the back toward the backside of the chicken yard, and the turkey is still after me.

422
00:25:31.034 --> 00:25:37.192
She can't really get me like she wants to, because my grandfather had clipped her wings,

423
00:25:37.192 --> 00:25:42.966
and that's the purpose of clipping the wing so they can’t fly over the fence.

424
00:25:42.966 --> 00:25:46.430
They can fly for only a little short distance.

425
00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:51.819
Anyway, my mother comes out, sees me, and she's out there, "Run baby run!"

426
00:25:51.819 --> 00:25:52.974
I'm wide open.

427
00:25:52.974 --> 00:25:55.668
That turkey still [?] coming to hit me!

428
00:25:55.668 --> 00:26:02.596
Look, when I come back around my mother inspired me I guess when she said, "Run baby run."

429
00:26:02.596 --> 00:26:08.755
I put a little gap on that turkey and come out that gate. [laughs] Oh, man.

430
00:26:08.755 --> 00:26:15.298
My mother . . . I was so glad my mother was there like, "God had save me" or whatever.

431
00:26:15.298 --> 00:26:22.996
She was there and gave me an apple to quiet me down because I was crying and scared to death.

432
00:26:22.996 --> 00:26:26.075
This is a true story on the real.

433
00:26:26.075 --> 00:26:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a good story.

434
00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:33.580
<v JACKSON>My grandfather's place . . . I mean my grandfather's turkey got on me.

435
00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:35.609
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a good story.

436
00:26:35.609 --> 00:26:36.116
<v JACKSON>Yes.

437
00:26:36.116 --> 00:26:37.130
It's real.

438
00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:41.188
<v ARSENEAULT>So what is your earliest memory of Mossville?

439
00:26:41.188 --> 00:26:41.696
<v JACKSON>Okay.

440
00:26:41.696 --> 00:26:48.797
When I came to Mossville, I was in the eighth grade when I moved.

441
00:26:48.797 --> 00:26:58.435
No, no. My earliest memory of Mossville is when I had to come here from Westlake . . . walk from Westlake.

442
00:26:58.435 --> 00:27:01.986
I'm fixing to be raw with you.

443
00:27:01.986 --> 00:27:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

444
00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:05.091
<v JACKSON>And tell you what it was.

445
00:27:05.091 --> 00:27:05.788
<v ARSENEAULT>I understand.

446
00:27:05.788 --> 00:27:07.879
I've heard some stories about Westlake.

447
00:27:07.879 --> 00:27:08.227
<v JACKSON>Yes.

448
00:27:08.227 --> 00:27:08.576
Okay.

449
00:27:08.576 --> 00:27:19.030
I lived only maybe less than a quarter of a mile from the swimming pool across the Ball Park and stuff because I couldn't go there because I was black.

450
00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:23.909
There had some other guys from Westlake and we had to walk here to . . .

451
00:27:23.909 --> 00:27:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How far was the walk?

452
00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:29.304
<v JACKSON>About . . . From Westlake to here about three miles at least.

453
00:27:29.304 --> 00:27:30.295
Three, maybe four.

454
00:27:30.295 --> 00:27:33.598
<v ARSENEAULT>And you had one right across the street from you?

455
00:27:33.598 --> 00:27:33.929
<v JACKSON>Yes.

456
00:27:33.929 --> 00:27:34.589
Swimming pool.

457
00:27:34.589 --> 00:27:37.893
A lot of the guys who lived in Westlake too,

458
00:27:37.893 --> 00:27:43.509
our parents were paying taxes like everybody else, but we couldn't go to the swimming pool there.

459
00:27:43.509 --> 00:27:45.161
Anyway, we would come here.

460
00:27:45.161 --> 00:27:52.098
That's my first memories of Mossville, coming to Mossville, walking to Mossville, going to the swimming pool which is right there.

461
00:27:52.098 --> 00:27:54.080
That's where I learned to swim.

462
00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.393
Have you talked to Blackie Payne yet?

463
00:27:56.393 --> 00:27:56.723
Eddie?

464
00:27:56.723 --> 00:28:00.357
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes he did an interview with his brothers and Ms. Barbara.

465
00:28:00.357 --> 00:28:01.018
<v JACKSON>Oh okay.

466
00:28:01.018 --> 00:28:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn't talk too much.

467
00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:03.874
<v JACKSON>Who, Blackie?

468
00:28:03.874 --> 00:28:06.059
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn't talk too much.

469
00:28:06.059 --> 00:28:06.933
<v JACKSON>He . . . Yes.

470
00:28:06.933 --> 00:28:08.681
<v ARSENEAULT>He was real quiet.

471
00:28:08.681 --> 00:28:10.429
<v JACKSON>That's pretty much him.

472
00:28:10.429 --> 00:28:14.361
We're good friends since when I first met him.

473
00:28:14.361 --> 00:28:20.042
He was one of the first guys from out here that I met.

474
00:28:20.042 --> 00:28:22.664
We were good friends ever since.

475
00:28:22.664 --> 00:28:26.597
This, I'm talking about being about eight years old.

476
00:28:26.597 --> 00:28:33.588
At that time they lived right where his family lives now across the street and stuff.

477
00:28:33.588 --> 00:28:43.639
They all knew how to swim, the Dellafosses, the Paynes . . . which Blackie is a Payne, and [Prides?], and Dolorous Prater and the Braxtons.

478
00:28:43.639 --> 00:28:55.000
They all knew [how to swim] because they would come at night and get in the swimming pool and just learn on their own self.

479
00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:56.203
<v ARSENEAULT>I hadn't heard that.

480
00:28:56.203 --> 00:28:57.407
They would sneak in?

481
00:28:57.407 --> 00:28:58.911
<v JACKSON>But when I came . . . Pardon?

482
00:28:58.911 --> 00:29:00.114
<v ARSENEAULT>They would sneak in?

483
00:29:00.114 --> 00:29:02.822
<v JACKSON>Sneak in across the fence, sneak in and swim.

484
00:29:02.822 --> 00:29:04.627
Lucky nobody got drowned or anything.

485
00:29:04.627 --> 00:29:11.847
When I came I was coming to take swimming lessons, me and the guys from Westlake and we had to take the swimming lessons.

486
00:29:11.847 --> 00:29:15.458
This dude named [Herb Fontenot?] was the life guard and swimming instructor.

487
00:29:15.458 --> 00:29:18.165
He had us doing . . . going through all the moves.

488
00:29:18.165 --> 00:29:19.369
<v ARSENEAULT>Getting on the board?

489
00:29:19.369 --> 00:29:22.076
<v JACKSON>No we didn't have to get on the board.

490
00:29:22.076 --> 00:29:22.678
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

491
00:29:22.678 --> 00:29:25.085
<v JACKSON>We didn't have any boards to get on.

492
00:29:25.085 --> 00:29:29.297
We were just holding on to the bank and stuff and kick our feet.

493
00:29:29.297 --> 00:29:33.208
Whatever the program was, we had to go through the stages and stuff.

494
00:29:33.208 --> 00:29:36.216
Teach you how to tread water and all of that.

495
00:29:36.216 --> 00:29:39.826
But and me and this girl is real good friends today, Dolorous,

496
00:29:39.826 --> 00:29:44.640
she was eight years old too at that time, but she could dive off the board.

497
00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:45.542
She could swim.

498
00:29:45.542 --> 00:29:48.551
She was one of the ones that would sneak in.

499
00:29:48.551 --> 00:29:53.064
I used to hate that girl, because she was on the side, laughing at us.

500
00:29:53.064 --> 00:30:00.886
She look, "Look at the little . . ." I think she called us the little rookies or something because we would be steady swimming going through the instructions,

501
00:30:00.886 --> 00:30:03.894
and they could already swim and dive off the board.

502
00:30:03.894 --> 00:30:11.525
"Look at the little rookies." [laughs] Anyway, that was my first good memories . . . I mean my memories from Mossville when I first came.

503
00:30:11.525 --> 00:30:20.625
Later on when I first came and then later on when I start to come to school here I was in the eighth grade and I came in.

504
00:30:20.625 --> 00:30:24.200
I went to school in Westlake, at this little elementary school.

505
00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:32.650
Then when I got in the seventh grade and was going to the eighth grade, eighth grade started here in Mossville and had to come here.

506
00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:35.250
That’s when I met a lot of people.

507
00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:37.525
A lot of different people and stuff,

508
00:30:37.525 --> 00:30:44.350
but I really knew Blackie Payne from the time that I used to come and swim . . . learn to swim and stuff.

509
00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:45.325
But that's it.

510
00:30:45.325 --> 00:30:47.925
That's my first real good memories of Mossville.

511
00:30:47.925 --> 00:30:51.500
As I . . . Went to school and stuff, played football, ran track.

512
00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:52.800
Had a great time.

513
00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:55.725
I have to tell you a track story now.

514
00:30:55.725 --> 00:30:56.375
This was . . .

515
00:30:56.375 --> 00:30:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was your coach?

516
00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:58.659
<v JACKSON>Coach LaSalle.

517
00:30:58.659 --> 00:30:59.319
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Williams?

518
00:30:59.319 --> 00:30:59.649
<v JACKSON>Yes.

519
00:30:59.649 --> 00:31:00.308
<v ARSENEAULT>Coach Williams.

520
00:31:00.308 --> 00:31:01.957
<v JACKSON>Yes, that's a great coach.

521
00:31:01.957 --> 00:31:03.276
He'll make you laugh.

522
00:31:03.276 --> 00:31:06.243
He could inspire you with his speeches and stuff.

523
00:31:06.243 --> 00:31:12.178
He'll cry and all of that when he inspiring you. [laughs] It was funny but it wasn't funny.

524
00:31:12.178 --> 00:31:17.124
We'll laugh about it afterwards, but he was serious when he be talking and stuff.

525
00:31:17.124 --> 00:31:19.103
He’d be so emotional and stuff.

526
00:31:19.103 --> 00:31:24.378
He'll cry when he talk telling you what he need you to do and all that.

527
00:31:24.378 --> 00:31:26.027
We played hard for him.

528
00:31:26.027 --> 00:31:28.995
So we won a lot of games and stuff.

529
00:31:28.995 --> 00:31:37.568
We lost state and I still just . . . Memory still haunts me when I think I could have . . . I know I could have made the right play.

530
00:31:37.568 --> 00:31:43.832
I was programmed to hit it . . . tackle instead of go for the ball and I could see this guy,

531
00:31:43.832 --> 00:31:47.459
the quarterback, he was about to throw it to a halfback.

532
00:31:47.459 --> 00:31:49.108
We had time running out.

533
00:31:49.108 --> 00:31:54.054
They had the ball but the quarterback, and I see what he's going to do.

534
00:31:54.054 --> 00:31:55.373
I'm playing defensive end.

535
00:31:55.373 --> 00:31:59.000
Still haunts me today that I made the wrong play.

536
00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:05.859
I see him go back with the ball to throw to the halfback that's running, like in the [flats?].

537
00:32:05.859 --> 00:32:11.634
I don't whether you know what the flats mean, but it's right out toward the sidelines.

538
00:32:11.634 --> 00:32:14.522
And I'm in position to get the ball.

539
00:32:14.522 --> 00:32:17.049
When he throws it, I see him.

540
00:32:17.049 --> 00:32:18.854
He's going to throw it.

541
00:32:18.854 --> 00:32:25.712
When he throws it, I could have stepped up and caught the ball and ran into the in zone,

542
00:32:25.712 --> 00:32:32.932
because we had them backed up maybe ten yards from the in zone, but instead I made the wrong play.

543
00:32:32.932 --> 00:32:34.376
I tackle a guy.

544
00:32:34.376 --> 00:32:37.985
I let him catch it, and then I tackle him.

545
00:32:37.985 --> 00:32:45.205
He didn't get anywhere, but time was running out with less than a minute and I made the wrong play.

546
00:32:45.205 --> 00:32:52.063
If I'd have gone for the ball I could have caught it, but I had been programmed to hit.

547
00:32:52.063 --> 00:32:53.507
That was my thing.

548
00:32:53.507 --> 00:32:53.868
Tackle.

549
00:32:53.868 --> 00:32:55.673
Don't let them get away.

550
00:32:55.673 --> 00:32:57.839
I didn't make the right move.

551
00:32:57.839 --> 00:33:04.337
Anyway, that's a story about football and we lost state eight to seven in the rain and mud.

552
00:33:04.337 --> 00:33:10.112
We was in a place called [ . . .?], somewhere in those woods back up around Baton Rouge [Louisiana].

553
00:33:10.112 --> 00:33:13.000
You ever heard of a school called [ . . .?]?

554
00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:13.804
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds familiar.

555
00:33:13.804 --> 00:33:17.019
<v ROGER>Well anyway it somewhere down around Baton Rouge.

556
00:33:17.019 --> 00:33:23.450
That was a . . . Now let me tell you, the track story, it was the real gem.

557
00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:29.077
This guy . . . We did a . . . Now that's one of my greatest memories at Mossville.

558
00:33:29.077 --> 00:33:35.507
We were . . . This guy, he's dead now, but the other three of us is still living.

559
00:33:35.507 --> 00:33:37.919
This guy name was [Esper Harris?].

560
00:33:37.919 --> 00:33:40.732
We were on the mile relay team.

561
00:33:40.732 --> 00:33:43.947
That's the last race in any track meet.

562
00:33:43.947 --> 00:33:47.163
We called it the four forty relay team.

563
00:33:47.163 --> 00:33:49.976
Well, it was a mile relay though.

564
00:33:49.976 --> 00:33:51.986
Lot of fast running around.

565
00:33:51.986 --> 00:33:55.201
Each person had to run one time around.

566
00:33:55.201 --> 00:33:57.612
That's a quarter of a mile.

567
00:33:57.612 --> 00:34:03.239
Anyway, there was Esper Harris, McKeever Edwards, myself, and this guy named Herman Moss.

568
00:34:03.239 --> 00:34:04.847
We called him Plug.

569
00:34:04.847 --> 00:34:10.072
He was the anchor man, the last person to run with the baton.

570
00:34:10.072 --> 00:34:14.493
We'd go to Lake Charles [Louisiana] to a dual track meet.

571
00:34:14.493 --> 00:34:17.708
You know what a dual track meet is?

572
00:34:17.708 --> 00:34:18.914
Only two people.

573
00:34:18.914 --> 00:34:20.923
I mean, only two schools.

574
00:34:20.923 --> 00:34:22.531
Like us and you.

575
00:34:22.531 --> 00:34:24.139
You go to [Boston?].

576
00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:25.344
I'm from Mossville.

577
00:34:25.344 --> 00:34:34.187
We go over to Boston, to run them, but we're not actually at Boston, but we're at what they call Wildcat Stadium.

578
00:34:34.187 --> 00:34:37.000
It was white stadium, white school.

579
00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:39.887
But we were there to run track that day.

580
00:34:39.887 --> 00:34:43.415
It was in the daytime so we could use their stadium.

581
00:34:43.415 --> 00:34:50.151
So we go in the evening after school in the dual track meet to go up against a triple A school.

582
00:34:50.151 --> 00:34:53.038
That's where my wife went to school, at Boston.

583
00:34:53.038 --> 00:34:59.132
So we go there, and Esper Harris is the lead man for our team, but when we get there,

584
00:34:59.132 --> 00:35:03.943
well, we know that before we even leave he didn't come to school that day,

585
00:35:03.943 --> 00:35:12.604
so we going to have to have another person run the leg . . . for the first leg for us against Boston when the race come in the end.

586
00:35:12.604 --> 00:35:15.811
We going to be the last people in the race.

587
00:35:15.811 --> 00:35:24.151
Boston had a great track team and they had a good relay team of four forty team. [noise of people in background] I hear my wife.

588
00:35:24.151 --> 00:35:26.075
She dressed in that seventies stuff.

589
00:35:26.075 --> 00:35:28.000
They like the flowerchild people.

590
00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:34.000
Anyway, we tell this . . . Coach gets this guy names Roy [Ingins?] who is the discus thrower.

591
00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.250
You know what a discus is?

592
00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:37.375
Okay, the discus.

593
00:35:37.375 --> 00:35:38.875
He's the discus thrower.

594
00:35:38.875 --> 00:35:42.250
He's not a sprinter like us and stuff, running.

595
00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:45.625
He's a real tall dude, long legs and stuff.

596
00:35:45.625 --> 00:35:54.250
Coach tell him and we tell him, “Ingins, we don't need you to try to out run this guy in the first leg.

597
00:35:54.250 --> 00:35:56.500
We know you not a sprinter.

598
00:35:56.500 --> 00:36:01.375
All you need to do is just kind of keep you a pace.

599
00:36:01.375 --> 00:36:07.750
Keep us in the race, but we know you not a track . . . sprinter to run with him.

600
00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:11.125
Just try to stay within striking distance of them.”

601
00:36:11.125 --> 00:36:11.875
“Okay, okay.”

602
00:36:11.875 --> 00:36:14.125
We tell him over and over.

603
00:36:14.125 --> 00:36:16.000
We passing the baton . . . practicing.

604
00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:18.250
Now it's time for the race.

605
00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:21.625
Ingins' going to run the first leg for us.

606
00:36:21.625 --> 00:36:23.875
McKeever, he's . . . We were all good.

607
00:36:23.875 --> 00:36:25.750
McKeever was the second guy.

608
00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:27.625
I was the third leg.

609
00:36:27.625 --> 00:36:32.500
Then Herman was the anchor and he was great as a track man.

610
00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:34.000
He was great.

611
00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:43.240
So, when they shoot the gun to start the race, Ingins takes off running like a jackrabbit. [laughs] He take off fast.

612
00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:51.220
Ingins take off fast, but we already told him, "Don't try to run fast, just keep up with them."

613
00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:57.520
Because you take off it’s . . . You got to go all the way around the track.

614
00:36:57.520 --> 00:37:02.560
That's a quarter of a mile and it's not no speed race.

615
00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:11.380
You got to pace yourself and then when you wait around and you're coming home you give it everything you got.

616
00:37:11.380 --> 00:37:22.300
But Ingins gives it everything he got from the start and about fifty yards down the track, the dude from Boston just slip past going on.

617
00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:24.400
The gap is opening up.

618
00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:27.340
Oh man, the people from Boston screaming.

619
00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:28.180
They fast.

620
00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:34.900
And look, then Ingins . . . The gap is really over a hundred yards when they come in.

621
00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:37.000
Over a hundred yards.

622
00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:45.453
Look, McKeever get the baton, all the people from Boston they fans is screaming and laughing because the gap is so large.

623
00:37:45.453 --> 00:37:50.447
Then McKeever he runs . . . he maybe gained twenty, twenty-five yards, on the dude.

624
00:37:50.447 --> 00:37:59.668
The lead is still tremendous. [This day?] when McKeever bring the baton to me, I'm at least seventy-five yards or so behind this guy.

625
00:37:59.668 --> 00:38:08.121
This is the third leg now, and I ran as hard as I ever ran in trying to close the gap some.

626
00:38:08.121 --> 00:38:15.805
Okay, when I come in, the dude was in front of me he done passed it to the last guy.

627
00:38:15.805 --> 00:38:17.726
The last guy is gone.

628
00:38:17.726 --> 00:38:20.032
He just took off . . . from Boston.

629
00:38:20.032 --> 00:38:23.874
Herman, he . . . I'm coming in and I'm just dog tired.

630
00:38:23.874 --> 00:38:27.332
I could hardly pick up my legs and stuff.

631
00:38:27.332 --> 00:38:29.253
Herman is like, "Come on."

632
00:38:29.253 --> 00:38:31.174
He looking down the track.

633
00:38:31.174 --> 00:38:33.095
He turned, "Come one, cat.

634
00:38:33.095 --> 00:38:33.863
Come on."

635
00:38:33.863 --> 00:38:36.937
Look, I stumble in there. [coughs] Excuse me.

636
00:38:36.937 --> 00:38:38.474
I stumble in, fall.

637
00:38:38.474 --> 00:38:40.011
I gave the baton.

638
00:38:40.011 --> 00:38:46.158
Actually, Herman came back about four or five feet and yanked it out of my hand.

639
00:38:46.158 --> 00:38:50.000
I fell down and I'm on the ground looking.

640
00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:54.526
The dude from Boston is gone into the first curve, going around.

641
00:38:54.526 --> 00:38:56.789
He about a hundred yards ahead.

642
00:38:56.789 --> 00:38:57.921
Herman take off.

643
00:38:57.921 --> 00:39:03.202
And what happens, the guy . . . Boston is screaming and hollering but Herman is running.

644
00:39:03.202 --> 00:39:06.596
I've never seen him run that fast that long.

645
00:39:06.596 --> 00:39:11.877
He . . . The dude just going around the . . . And then he hit the back stretch.

646
00:39:11.877 --> 00:39:14.895
He hit the back stretch, Herman is gaining.

647
00:39:14.895 --> 00:39:16.026
You could see.

648
00:39:16.026 --> 00:39:19.798
Closing the gap on him, and Herman's still running hard.

649
00:39:19.798 --> 00:39:22.816
I'm thinking he can't keep up this pace.

650
00:39:22.816 --> 00:39:29.982
Herman can't for that long because that's a whole quarter of a mile he's trying to catch this guy.

651
00:39:29.982 --> 00:39:34.886
What is happening, Herman is closing the gap the people are still screaming,

652
00:39:34.886 --> 00:39:42.053
but they trying to tell him that he need to look back because this dude is coming at him.

653
00:39:42.053 --> 00:39:50.351
They just hollering and this dude, he not into a full run because he don't . . . the gap is so far he thinks.

654
00:39:50.351 --> 00:39:56.763
But Herman is still coming and nobody . . . We couldn't understand how Herman could keep up that pace.

655
00:39:56.763 --> 00:40:00.158
He always ran with a cross in his mouth.

656
00:40:00.158 --> 00:40:04.307
Not a cross, a chain that had a cross on it.

657
00:40:04.307 --> 00:40:09.588
He put the chain in his mouth just to keep spit in his mouth.

658
00:40:09.588 --> 00:40:16.000
A metal chain like that would help to keep spit in his mouth when he’d run.

659
00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:19.188
So, when they come into the last curve,

660
00:40:19.188 --> 00:40:25.962
Herman is like . . . The dude realize Herman is back there close, maybe about twenty yards from him.

661
00:40:25.962 --> 00:40:29.549
I couldn't believe it man, still on the ground.

662
00:40:29.549 --> 00:40:33.932
Herman is dead close on this dude and he looked back.

663
00:40:33.932 --> 00:40:37.120
He see him and the crowd is hollering.

664
00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:42.699
Look, they coming down toward . . . They about . . . When they come into the last curve,

665
00:40:42.699 --> 00:40:51.865
a hundred yards from the finish, they hit the last curve so they got about eighty yards to go to the finish line.

666
00:40:51.865 --> 00:40:56.647
Herman is right after them, maybe about five feet behind him now.

667
00:40:56.647 --> 00:40:58.241
Herman head is back.

668
00:40:58.241 --> 00:40:59.038
He's straining.

669
00:40:59.038 --> 00:41:01.429
He's straining to catch this guy.

670
00:41:01.429 --> 00:41:03.820
The kids just got . . . And look.

671
00:41:03.820 --> 00:41:06.211
This what you will not believe.

672
00:41:06.211 --> 00:41:09.000
This was . . . Well it wasn't for money.

673
00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.391
Wasn't for a trophy or medals.

674
00:41:11.391 --> 00:41:14.579
It was just trying to beat this dude.

675
00:41:14.579 --> 00:41:20.556
Herman’s run was so intense and so dramatic that their students was hollering, "Catch him!

676
00:41:20.556 --> 00:41:22.947
Catch him!" [laughs] It was unreal.

677
00:41:22.947 --> 00:41:25.338
Their students was hollering, "Catch him!

678
00:41:25.338 --> 00:41:26.135
Catch him!"

679
00:41:26.135 --> 00:41:29.722
When they go to the . . . They never do this.

680
00:41:29.722 --> 00:41:31.714
Put a tape across that.

681
00:41:31.714 --> 00:41:34.504
Not this race but those other sprints.

682
00:41:34.504 --> 00:41:40.880
They see it was going to be close so they stretch a . . . What you call it?

683
00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:48.850
Just a little piece of like a little ribbon across right at the finish line for whoever come across first,

684
00:41:48.850 --> 00:41:54.827
the tape is going to be on them . . . if you lean into it or whatever.

685
00:41:54.827 --> 00:42:00.406
Look, when they crossed the line Herman falls . . . Our little . . . You talked to [T.

686
00:42:00.406 --> 00:42:01.203
Porter?] yet?

687
00:42:01.203 --> 00:42:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

688
00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:02.343
<v JACKSON>Okay.

689
00:42:02.343 --> 00:42:06.807
His brother, we call him Pork Chop, he was a trainer for us.

690
00:42:06.807 --> 00:42:08.867
He was a little bitty guy.

691
00:42:08.867 --> 00:42:14.018
Little Pork Chop was on his knees at the end of the race like this.

692
00:42:14.018 --> 00:42:15.392
Herman crosses the line.

693
00:42:15.392 --> 00:42:17.108
The tape is on Herman.

694
00:42:17.108 --> 00:42:19.169
Herman fall into Pork Chop’s arms.

695
00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:21.916
The dude from Boston, I mean, he loses.

696
00:42:21.916 --> 00:42:28.783
When Herman falls in the Pork Chop's arms, little Pork Chop spin him over and start working on his legs.

697
00:42:28.783 --> 00:42:32.217
Charlie horses is in his legs cramping him and stuff.

698
00:42:32.217 --> 00:42:33.247
But he won.

699
00:42:33.247 --> 00:42:34.620
It was dramatic, man.

700
00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:39.084
It was the . . . To see those people hollering for, excuse me, "Catch him!

701
00:42:39.084 --> 00:42:39.771
Catch him!"

702
00:42:39.771 --> 00:42:41.831
It turned the whole thing around.

703
00:42:41.831 --> 00:42:49.386
Their own people was hollering for Herman to catch him because he had run so hard to . . . And he caught that guy.

704
00:42:49.386 --> 00:42:50.072
Beat him.

705
00:42:50.072 --> 00:42:53.506
<v ARSENEAULT>I've actually read about McKeever in some of the papers.

706
00:42:53.506 --> 00:42:54.193
<v JACKSON>Oh Yes?

707
00:42:54.193 --> 00:42:56.253
<v ARSENEAULT>He won a lot of awards.

708
00:42:56.253 --> 00:42:57.970
<v JACKSON>Well the preacher or McKeever . . .?

709
00:42:57.970 --> 00:42:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>McKeever Edwards.

710
00:42:59.000 --> 00:42:59.500
<v JACKSON>Oh, okay.

711
00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:01.250
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that the one you're talking about?

712
00:43:01.250 --> 00:43:01.500
<v JACKSON>Yes.

713
00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:03.250
<v ARSENEAULT>Won an award for football I think.

714
00:43:03.250 --> 00:43:06.250
<v JACKSON>Yes, he played football with us, with me, and him, and Blunt.

715
00:43:06.250 --> 00:43:06.500
Yes.

716
00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh y'all all played together?

717
00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:08.356
<v JACKSON>Yes.

718
00:43:08.356 --> 00:43:10.491
McKeever was a good football player.

719
00:43:10.491 --> 00:43:11.203
Blunt too.

720
00:43:11.203 --> 00:43:13.338
We always had a good team.

721
00:43:13.338 --> 00:43:14.761
They were offensive players.

722
00:43:14.761 --> 00:43:16.185
McKeever was and Blunt.

723
00:43:16.185 --> 00:43:17.252
I played defense.

724
00:43:17.252 --> 00:43:19.387
We always had a good team.

725
00:43:19.387 --> 00:43:23.302
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever go to any of the canteens in Mossville?

726
00:43:23.302 --> 00:43:25.793
<v JACKSON>Yes, I had . . . Freelow had a canteen.

727
00:43:25.793 --> 00:43:31.842
It was close to school and then there was one right across the street from the school.

728
00:43:31.842 --> 00:43:34.333
Yes, we went there mostly on Sundays.

729
00:43:34.333 --> 00:43:36.113
<v ARSENEAULT>What did y'all do there?

730
00:43:36.113 --> 00:43:36.468
<v JACKSON>Dance.

731
00:43:36.468 --> 00:43:38.248
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that what it was?

732
00:43:38.248 --> 00:43:39.671
<v JACKSON>Talk to the girls.

733
00:43:39.671 --> 00:43:41.095
That's what I did.

734
00:43:41.095 --> 00:43:42.874
That's what it was about.

735
00:43:42.874 --> 00:43:43.230
Yes.

736
00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:44.297
As a guy.

737
00:43:44.297 --> 00:43:44.653
Yes.

738
00:43:44.653 --> 00:43:46.077
You go on Sunday.

739
00:43:46.077 --> 00:43:53.550
Put on your little starched khaki jeans . . . khaki pants and a nice ironed shirt and stuff and have your shoes shined.

740
00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:54.973
Going to the canteen.

741
00:43:54.973 --> 00:43:55.685
Kicking around.

742
00:43:55.685 --> 00:43:59.599
I had . . . I don't know if you talked to him yet,

743
00:43:59.599 --> 00:44:06.360
but he had a car when we were in our last year and things. [Owen Key?] had a mustang.

744
00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:09.563
We'd pick up plenty of girls in the mustang.

745
00:44:09.563 --> 00:44:12.054
We had a lot of good times.

746
00:44:12.054 --> 00:44:17.392
I won't go into all of that, but we had a lot of good times.

747
00:44:17.392 --> 00:44:19.171
He'll probably be here tonight.

748
00:44:19.171 --> 00:44:22.730
I talked to him earlier, he said he was coming.

749
00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:25.577
I don't think you talked to him yet.

750
00:44:25.577 --> 00:44:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What's his name?

751
00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:27.802
<v JACKSON>Owen [Kayhe?].

752
00:44:27.802 --> 00:44:28.203
Yes.

753
00:44:28.203 --> 00:44:32.615
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of music did y'all listen to in the canteen?

754
00:44:32.615 --> 00:44:38.231
<v JACKSON>Music by . . . Well you could call it rock, rock and roll music and stuff.

755
00:44:38.231 --> 00:44:45.451
By like Chuck Garry, and James Brown, and this guy Al Green. [ . . . ?] the singers and things like that.

756
00:44:45.451 --> 00:44:46.253
Bennie King.

757
00:44:46.253 --> 00:44:46.654
B.B.

758
00:44:46.654 --> 00:44:48.659
King for the blues stand.

759
00:44:48.659 --> 00:44:49.060
Yes.

760
00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:52.269
Had a lot of good music back then.

761
00:44:52.269 --> 00:44:55.478
A lot of people could dance real good.

762
00:44:55.478 --> 00:44:58.687
Swing out, it's a fast dance and stuff.

763
00:44:58.687 --> 00:45:06.709
This little guy, he's still alive too, him and his sister they used to dance a lot together, swing out.

764
00:45:06.709 --> 00:45:07.912
They were great.

765
00:45:07.912 --> 00:45:09.115
His name's [Willie?].

766
00:45:09.115 --> 00:45:09.918
Willie Jasmine.

767
00:45:09.918 --> 00:45:11.923
His sister's name was Shirley.

768
00:45:11.923 --> 00:45:13.929
They could dance real good.

769
00:45:13.929 --> 00:45:17.940
My brother and my sister could dance good together too.

770
00:45:17.940 --> 00:45:22.753
We were growing up, used to go to the canteen and stuff.

771
00:45:22.753 --> 00:45:31.978
My sister could be doing something in the kitchen or in the house with the broom or something and the music come on,

772
00:45:31.978 --> 00:45:37.593
my brother just grab her and throw the broom away and they get going.

773
00:45:37.593 --> 00:45:40.000
It was great times, yes.

774
00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:48.625
It was like not being rich, whatever, but we was surviving, like a lot of families was struggling and stuff.

775
00:45:48.625 --> 00:45:59.838
We didn't see the thing as us being poor, because we had a lot of fun, a lot of love in the family still and things.

776
00:45:59.838 --> 00:46:01.131
I don't know.

777
00:46:01.131 --> 00:46:03.719
People looked out for each other.

778
00:46:03.719 --> 00:46:11.481
Things with . . . you can count on people if somebody was down or having some problems in the home,

779
00:46:11.481 --> 00:46:14.500
money problems, or grocery problems, or whatever.

780
00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:17.950
Somebody would kick in and help them out.

781
00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:20.538
You could leave your door open.

782
00:46:20.538 --> 00:46:27.006
The people wouldn't break in, steal, like go in your house and stuff like that.

783
00:46:27.006 --> 00:46:33.044
If somebody was doing that they'd get told on and taken care of quick.

784
00:46:33.044 --> 00:46:37.356
It was a time when them things started to change.

785
00:46:37.356 --> 00:46:41.669
As the years went by, people did more unlawful things.

786
00:46:41.669 --> 00:46:45.981
That’s just the way it goes, it roll like that.

787
00:46:45.981 --> 00:46:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any clubs in Mossville?

788
00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:49.647
<v JACKSON>Yes.

789
00:46:49.647 --> 00:46:58.059
It was one especially called Cowboy Inn, but that was like the cowboys.

790
00:46:58.059 --> 00:47:04.529
There was a lot . . . I didn't really go there because . . .

791
00:47:04.529 --> 00:47:08.412
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you mean the cowboys?

792
00:47:08.412 --> 00:47:11.000
Like actual cowboys?

793
00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:11.372
<v JACKSON>Rough.

794
00:47:11.372 --> 00:47:12.860
No, I mean rough.

795
00:47:12.860 --> 00:47:20.298
People carried guns and there was a lot of shootings going on and fights, and people being cut and stabbed.

796
00:47:20.298 --> 00:47:23.645
It was real, like the old west type places.

797
00:47:23.645 --> 00:47:25.876
It was called the Cowboy Inn.

798
00:47:25.876 --> 00:47:31.083
The Cowboy Club, or the Cowboy . . . I think it was called the Cowboy Club.

799
00:47:31.083 --> 00:47:31.455
Yes.

800
00:47:31.455 --> 00:47:34.058
A dude ran it named Peter [Deville?].

801
00:47:34.058 --> 00:47:41.496
Like ride his horse up into the place. [laughs] He had a horse that he'd ride up in this club.

802
00:47:41.496 --> 00:47:42.983
That kind of stuff.

803
00:47:42.983 --> 00:47:44.471
That wasn't my shot.

804
00:47:44.471 --> 00:47:47.818
Wouldn’t take a girl to a place like that.

805
00:47:47.818 --> 00:47:50.050
But there was the main club.

806
00:47:50.050 --> 00:47:57.488
Then they had a club that I never gone to, back this way somewhere around where T. Porter lives now.

807
00:47:57.488 --> 00:47:59.719
It was called The Swamp House.

808
00:47:59.719 --> 00:48:03.066
I've never been there since . . . ever in my life.

809
00:48:03.066 --> 00:48:05.669
I think this club is still there.

810
00:48:05.669 --> 00:48:07.901
They may be closed down now.

811
00:48:07.901 --> 00:48:10.504
They had another club called Joy Hill.

812
00:48:10.504 --> 00:48:13.851
It was kind of in between Mossville and Westlake.

813
00:48:13.851 --> 00:48:15.339
I never went there.

814
00:48:15.339 --> 00:48:19.802
That was . . . I was a kid really growing up at that time.

815
00:48:19.802 --> 00:48:26.868
Those older clubs like Joy Hill and Swamp House . . . But I was a teenager when Cowboy club was opened.

816
00:48:26.868 --> 00:48:27.983
The Cowboy Inn.

817
00:48:27.983 --> 00:48:30.959
That's what it was called, The Cowboy Inn.

818
00:48:30.959 --> 00:48:32.446
I wouldn't go there.

819
00:48:32.446 --> 00:48:37.281
That's about it for clubs at that time when I was coming up.

820
00:48:37.281 --> 00:48:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What else did y'all do for fun growing up?

821
00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:44.960
<v JACKSON>Rode bicycles, fixed bicycles and like put them . . . made bicycles.

822
00:48:44.960 --> 00:48:48.523
Like go get you some parts from the junkyard.

823
00:48:48.523 --> 00:48:53.275
You may get a frame from the junkyard and paint it up.

824
00:48:53.275 --> 00:49:01.987
You had to paint . . . The only thing you couldn’t probably find good. . . You could find rims at the junkyard and a frame.

825
00:49:01.987 --> 00:49:05.946
You know what the frame is on a bicycle, right?

826
00:49:05.946 --> 00:49:10.302
You had the frame and you could find your rims sometimes.

827
00:49:10.302 --> 00:49:13.866
Most of the times you had to buy tires.

828
00:49:13.866 --> 00:49:19.013
You put them together and from an old bike or put bikes together.

829
00:49:19.013 --> 00:49:24.557
Everybody going to make . . . You're the only one going to have a good bicycle.

830
00:49:24.557 --> 00:49:26.933
Everybody got a bike but you.

831
00:49:26.933 --> 00:49:34.852
So you go to the junkyard, get parts and somebody got some here, some there, you got some in between.

832
00:49:34.852 --> 00:49:38.020
We’d make a bicycle and then paint it.

833
00:49:38.020 --> 00:49:39.208
There you go.

834
00:49:39.208 --> 00:49:40.000
Yes.

835
00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:45.036
Let me tell you another story about when I messed up growing up in Westlake.

836
00:49:45.036 --> 00:49:47.051
This girl is still alive today.

837
00:49:47.051 --> 00:49:49.066
My mother was alive then too.

838
00:49:49.066 --> 00:49:51.752
She had bought me a brand new bicycle.

839
00:49:51.752 --> 00:49:55.109
Well, we lived across the street from the Catholic Church.

840
00:49:55.109 --> 00:50:01.489
Was maybe a quarter of a mile from where all the rest of the black people lived in Westlake.

841
00:50:01.489 --> 00:50:07.197
We lived down the street from them right in front of Saint John Bosco, the new church.

842
00:50:07.197 --> 00:50:12.234
I wanted to go and show my bike off to my friends down the street.

843
00:50:12.234 --> 00:50:14.248
It was late in the evening.

844
00:50:14.248 --> 00:50:17.270
My grandmother . . . My mother told me not to go.

845
00:50:17.270 --> 00:50:21.971
To go wait until tomorrow because it was almost . . . It was coming towards sundown.

846
00:50:21.971 --> 00:50:23.314
But I didn't listen.

847
00:50:23.314 --> 00:50:26.000
I couldn't wait to show it off.

848
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:27.407
So I slip out,

849
00:50:27.407 --> 00:50:33.741
and go out through the backyard and go through these white people . . . Mr. Jeans . . . whatever his name was.

850
00:50:33.741 --> 00:50:35.500
We call him Mr. Jeans.

851
00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.259
He was a white man.

852
00:50:37.259 --> 00:50:46.407
And we slip out through his yard and ran around and I went down to the . . . where the majority of the black people stayed in Westlake,

853
00:50:46.407 --> 00:50:47.463
on Grant Street.

854
00:50:47.463 --> 00:50:49.574
Anyway, my friends there and stuff.

855
00:50:49.574 --> 00:50:51.685
They see my bicycle and stuff.

856
00:50:51.685 --> 00:50:55.204
I'm telling them how fast it can go and stuff.

857
00:50:55.204 --> 00:50:59.074
I said, "Let me show you how fast it can go."

858
00:50:59.074 --> 00:51:00.833
The road was gravel then.

859
00:51:00.833 --> 00:51:01.537
So okay.

860
00:51:01.537 --> 00:51:04.000
Look, I take off on the bike.

861
00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:09.630
I take off on the bicycle and . . . Brenda Bernard is a good friend of mine too.

862
00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:13.852
She was a real pretty girl when we were small and stuff.

863
00:51:13.852 --> 00:51:19.481
This is kind of deviating from the story, but I used to call her straight legs.

864
00:51:19.481 --> 00:51:20.889
She was real pretty.

865
00:51:20.889 --> 00:51:23.000
Had them long, pretty legs.

866
00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:26.721
Anyway, her and this girl named Viola [Marlboro?].

867
00:51:26.721 --> 00:51:29.512
They're playing hopscotch in Brenda's yard.

868
00:51:29.512 --> 00:51:36.488
But it . . . Brenda's family . . . In the driveway but the driveway has . . . What you call these?

869
00:51:36.488 --> 00:51:41.605
It's not . . . You don’t all it weeds . . . shrubs . . . like some shrubbery,

870
00:51:41.605 --> 00:51:48.581
but you can’t see unless you come all the way out on to the street.

871
00:51:48.581 --> 00:51:51.372
The shrubbery was in the driveway.

872
00:51:51.372 --> 00:51:56.953
So I'm riding the bicycle down, showing them how fast it's got.

873
00:51:56.953 --> 00:52:03.000
I take off and go down the street this way going real fast.

874
00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:08.116
And Brenda and Viola playing hopscotch, but I don't know this.

875
00:52:08.116 --> 00:52:16.488
But when I get right about Brenda's house by the driveway Viola comes out of the driveway backwards.

876
00:52:16.488 --> 00:52:17.884
Jumped out backwards.

877
00:52:17.884 --> 00:52:19.279
I hit it.

878
00:52:19.279 --> 00:52:20.674
Right into her.

879
00:52:20.674 --> 00:52:23.000
Bam with the bike.

880
00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:24.321
It was dreadful though.

881
00:52:24.321 --> 00:52:27.625
Look, I hit her and she go falls . . . goes down.

882
00:52:27.625 --> 00:52:29.277
I get the . . . I'm sliding.

883
00:52:29.277 --> 00:52:33.571
When I hit her, I fall off the bike and the bike slides.

884
00:52:33.571 --> 00:52:36.545
My arm and hands just on the gravel road.

885
00:52:36.545 --> 00:52:38.196
I'm cut up and stuff.

886
00:52:38.196 --> 00:52:42.491
By the time I finished sliding, Brenda is screaming and I look up.

887
00:52:42.491 --> 00:52:44.804
I tried to pick up the bike.

888
00:52:44.804 --> 00:52:46.125
The guys running down.

889
00:52:46.125 --> 00:52:49.759
Brenda is screaming and Viola bleeding from the nose and stuff.

890
00:52:49.759 --> 00:52:51.080
She's a little girl.

891
00:52:51.080 --> 00:52:53.393
We both eight years old or something.

892
00:52:53.393 --> 00:52:56.036
I done cracked into her with the bicycle.

893
00:52:56.036 --> 00:52:57.357
Guess what I do?

894
00:52:57.357 --> 00:52:59.009
Tell me what I did.

895
00:52:59.009 --> 00:53:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Run home?

896
00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:00.684
<v JACKSON>Took off.

897
00:53:00.684 --> 00:53:04.103
Hit and run. [laughs] Hit and run and left her.

898
00:53:04.103 --> 00:53:04.787
Went home.

899
00:53:04.787 --> 00:53:07.523
The bad news had beat me home though.

900
00:53:07.523 --> 00:53:10.258
The old people had to call already, man.

901
00:53:10.258 --> 00:53:16.071
That's the worst my mother ever whipped me, because see I used to do things and stuff,

902
00:53:16.071 --> 00:53:21.884
but I was a good kid in the sense I could say speeches and things and stuff.

903
00:53:21.884 --> 00:53:28.723
My mother read them to me, like three or four times, and she'd say, "I want you to say this."

904
00:53:28.723 --> 00:53:35.561
It wasn’t them real long speeches and things, but for a little kid I could pick up parts of it.

905
00:53:35.561 --> 00:53:38.981
If she'd give me speeches, I would really learn something.

906
00:53:38.981 --> 00:53:43.768
Anyway, when I would do something, she would whack me a couple of times.

907
00:53:43.768 --> 00:53:46.845
I'd holler real loud and she'd let me go.

908
00:53:46.845 --> 00:53:48.555
I was always into something.

909
00:53:48.555 --> 00:53:50.606
Anyway, that day, she wasn't playing.

910
00:53:50.606 --> 00:53:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>About to get mad over it.

911
00:53:53.000 --> 00:53:56.791
<v JACKSON>I had to . . . I broke away from her and ran outside.

912
00:53:56.791 --> 00:54:01.615
Look, but the scene was . . . The girl had to be taken to the hospital.

913
00:54:01.615 --> 00:54:02.994
She developed blood clots.

914
00:54:02.994 --> 00:54:03.683
Almost died.

915
00:54:03.683 --> 00:54:07.129
It's a little bitty girl and I had wrecked her.

916
00:54:07.129 --> 00:54:07.818
Hit her.

917
00:54:07.818 --> 00:54:10.920
It was . . . Messed her chest up too and stuff.

918
00:54:10.920 --> 00:54:13.332
Blood clots had formed in her chest.

919
00:54:13.332 --> 00:54:15.055
Anyway, she survived it though.

920
00:54:15.055 --> 00:54:17.468
That day my mother was whipping me.

921
00:54:17.468 --> 00:54:23.326
I broke away from her and she could have had my brothers catch me, but she didn't.

922
00:54:23.326 --> 00:54:25.394
I ran and I went outside.

923
00:54:25.394 --> 00:54:26.772
It was dark then.

924
00:54:26.772 --> 00:54:32.631
I sit on the steps and stuff, and finally she came and said, "Come on in baby.

925
00:54:32.631 --> 00:54:36.422
I'm through whipping you, but I got to talk to you."

926
00:54:36.422 --> 00:54:44.692
Then well when I went inside she talked to me and stuff and told me what . . . she showed me the consequences of not listening.

927
00:54:44.692 --> 00:54:51.929
What this girl is hurt bad, and we'll probably have to pay some doctor bills for her and stuff like that.

928
00:54:51.929 --> 00:54:58.132
Anyway, it was . . . I never messed up again, but my mother didn't live but for another two years.

929
00:54:58.132 --> 00:55:04.680
I never messed up again . . . from what she told me, when I was a little kid at that time.

930
00:55:04.680 --> 00:55:06.748
Yes, that was a dreadful time.

931
00:55:06.748 --> 00:55:08.126
That girl's still living.

932
00:55:08.126 --> 00:55:13.985
We saw each other about . . . maybe about . . . They had a . . . It was in January of this year.

933
00:55:13.985 --> 00:55:17.086
They had a thing for Coach LaSalle [Williams] here.

934
00:55:17.086 --> 00:55:18.809
Did you hear about it?

935
00:55:18.809 --> 00:55:22.600
Okay, I came home for that and I saw her there.

936
00:55:22.600 --> 00:55:23.634
She was there.

937
00:55:23.634 --> 00:55:25.357
So we talked about that.

938
00:55:25.357 --> 00:55:27.080
We was laughing about it.

939
00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:33.972
Her husband was telling me, “Yeah, my wife told me you almost . . . She almost didn't make it because of you.”

940
00:55:33.972 --> 00:55:38.797
I said, "Yes she telling you the truth, because I definitely ran over her."

941
00:55:38.797 --> 00:55:45.000
Yes, it was a lovely, exciting time in my life, but I did a lot of things.

942
00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:49.982
I'm really blessed to be here, because during the time I was growing up,

943
00:55:49.982 --> 00:55:55.320
this is all before . . . So many things happened before my mother passed and stuff happened.

944
00:55:55.320 --> 00:55:58.523
But, I got an ice pick in my foot.

945
00:55:58.523 --> 00:56:04.217
I threw the ice pick into my foot, my own self, throwing it on the floor.

946
00:56:04.217 --> 00:56:06.352
Just sticking in the wood floor.

947
00:56:06.352 --> 00:56:08.843
Throw the ice pick through my foot.

948
00:56:08.843 --> 00:56:10.979
I fell out of a tree.

949
00:56:10.979 --> 00:56:17.028
That's why they gave me the name [Cat?] because I used to climb trees all that time.

950
00:56:17.028 --> 00:56:20.943
This time I fell out the tree I didn't hurt myself.

951
00:56:20.943 --> 00:56:24.502
I didn't break anything, but I was a little guy.

952
00:56:24.502 --> 00:56:26.281
I fell out that tree.

953
00:56:26.281 --> 00:56:28.772
I stuck a pencil in my throat.

954
00:56:28.772 --> 00:56:30.907
My mother . . . Now this was crazy.

955
00:56:30.907 --> 00:56:35.534
I had the pencil, playing around, and it got lodged in my throat.

956
00:56:35.534 --> 00:56:38.737
My father and mother trying to get it out.

957
00:56:38.737 --> 00:56:44.075
They had this kerosene lamp that my father had me leaning back . . . Why you laughing?

958
00:56:44.075 --> 00:56:45.498
But any . . . It's funny?

959
00:56:45.498 --> 00:56:46.210
I guess.

960
00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:48.701
But this all is real, all true.

961
00:56:48.701 --> 00:56:50.836
My father . . . My mother holding me.

962
00:56:50.836 --> 00:56:54.751
My father just . . . He catches it, and he pull it out.

963
00:56:54.751 --> 00:56:59.021
As he pulling it, it kind of hurt and then I kicked.

964
00:56:59.021 --> 00:57:06.139
When I kicked, I kicked the lamp out of his hand and it falls on my leg and burned me.

965
00:57:06.139 --> 00:57:11.121
That burn stayed there for . . . I know a good . . . I'm sixty-eight years old now.

966
00:57:11.121 --> 00:57:15.036
That burn maybe left off of me maybe five years ago.

967
00:57:15.036 --> 00:57:15.747
Finally faded.

968
00:57:15.747 --> 00:57:22.509
But it was like a crescent shape, like a moon shaped thing on my leg for a long time.

969
00:57:22.509 --> 00:57:25.000
That was one of the things.

970
00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:28.182
I got my eye shot out with a bow and arrow.

971
00:57:28.182 --> 00:57:30.786
My brother shot me with the bow and arrow.

972
00:57:30.786 --> 00:57:32.811
That's why I got this patch on.

973
00:57:32.811 --> 00:57:35.994
I didn't . . . I lost the sight in it at that time.

974
00:57:35.994 --> 00:57:37.151
This is what happened.

975
00:57:37.151 --> 00:57:38.019
My daddy told.

976
00:57:38.019 --> 00:57:40.044
We had this homemade bow and arrow.

977
00:57:40.044 --> 00:57:42.069
It's me and my two brothers playing.

978
00:57:42.069 --> 00:57:43.805
I'm the youngest out of them.

979
00:57:43.805 --> 00:57:45.830
Now this is the second oldest boy.

980
00:57:45.830 --> 00:57:47.566
It was . . . His name was Billy.

981
00:57:47.566 --> 00:57:49.013
My other brother was Karl.

982
00:57:49.013 --> 00:57:50.459
My oldest brother was [Donny?].

983
00:57:50.459 --> 00:57:53.063
But this was with me and Billy and Karl.

984
00:57:53.063 --> 00:57:54.799
We were playing in the yard.

985
00:57:54.799 --> 00:57:58.849
Father come home from work he see us with this old bow and arrow.

986
00:57:58.849 --> 00:58:00.874
And he takes it, in the house.

987
00:58:00.874 --> 00:58:04.635
He tell us like, "One of y'all will probably get hurt with this.

988
00:58:04.635 --> 00:58:05.792
Give me this thing."

989
00:58:05.792 --> 00:58:08.107
He takes it and go in the house.

990
00:58:08.107 --> 00:58:09.553
He's just coming from work.

991
00:58:09.553 --> 00:58:11.000
We in the yard.

992
00:58:11.000 --> 00:58:16.774
So my brother's know my mother let me have my way a lot of times so they talked me into,

993
00:58:16.774 --> 00:58:19.950
"Go ask Mother if you can get the bow and arrow.

994
00:58:19.950 --> 00:58:21.682
She'll probably give it to you."

995
00:58:21.682 --> 00:58:24.280
I go and ask for the bow and arrow.

996
00:58:24.280 --> 00:58:26.301
Later on she gives it to me.

997
00:58:26.301 --> 00:58:31.209
Tell me not to get dirty because she give me a bath when I go in there.

998
00:58:31.209 --> 00:58:34.385
I'm about . . . I guess about four or five at the time.

999
00:58:34.385 --> 00:58:41.891
When I go in she give me a bath so tell me not to get dirty, “Okay, you can go back outside but don't get dirty.”

1000
00:58:41.891 --> 00:58:42.757
I say, "Alright."

1001
00:58:42.757 --> 00:58:45.933
So I go give the bow and arrow to my brother.

1002
00:58:45.933 --> 00:58:52.573
Then this is particular thing about it—he doesn't shoot it like this on the level; he shoots it straight up in the air.

1003
00:58:52.573 --> 00:58:53.728
I'm across the yard,

1004
00:58:53.728 --> 00:58:59.791
siting on this tire that a tree is growing out of . . . or something my grandfather had planted a long time ago,

1005
00:58:59.791 --> 00:59:00.946
like a tractor tire.

1006
00:59:00.946 --> 00:59:02.100
I'm sitting on it.

1007
00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:04.410
He shoots it straight up in the air.

1008
00:59:04.410 --> 00:59:05.565
The wind takes it.

1009
00:59:05.565 --> 00:59:07.586
I see it, it’s way up there.

1010
00:59:07.586 --> 00:59:11.628
The wind takes it and it drifts over and is just going like this.

1011
00:59:11.628 --> 00:59:13.360
It looked like it hypnotized me.

1012
00:59:13.360 --> 00:59:15.958
I could see it coming, but I don't move.

1013
00:59:15.958 --> 00:59:17.690
It fall right into my eye.

1014
00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:17.979
Bam.

1015
00:59:17.979 --> 00:59:20.000
What are the chances of that?

1016
00:59:20.000 --> 00:59:21.502
My mother was outside again.

1017
00:59:21.502 --> 00:59:22.704
I was screaming murder.

1018
00:59:22.704 --> 00:59:23.606
That was hurting.

1019
00:59:23.606 --> 00:59:27.512
Went right through here, through the top through the bottom of my eye.

1020
00:59:27.512 --> 00:59:31.118
And I broke it off, because it was nothing but a weed.

1021
00:59:31.118 --> 00:59:32.320
But it was sharpened.

1022
00:59:32.320 --> 00:59:33.823
You know what I'm saying?

1023
00:59:33.823 --> 00:59:39.532
A sharpened weed would be a . . . because of the board that was on me . . . the arrow was on me.

1024
00:59:39.532 --> 00:59:44.039
I mean, we had pulled out the ground sharpened it up and we shooting it.

1025
00:59:44.039 --> 00:59:44.941
Anyway, that happened.

1026
00:59:44.941 --> 00:59:47.044
My mother took me to the hospital.

1027
00:59:47.044 --> 00:59:50.650
I didn't lose my eye per se then, and then I did.

1028
00:59:50.650 --> 00:59:52.453
I lost the sight in it.

1029
00:59:52.453 --> 00:59:54.256
I couldn't see out of it.

1030
00:59:54.256 --> 00:59:57.562
That was when I was real little, about four or five.

1031
00:59:57.562 --> 01:00:00.266
I had to tell them to take it out.

1032
01:00:00.266 --> 01:00:03.872
When I was eighteen, I got hit in it again, playing football.

1033
01:00:03.872 --> 01:00:06.276
One of the guys from here did it.

1034
01:00:06.276 --> 01:00:08.079
He dead now too, that [ . . .?] Prater.

1035
01:00:08.079 --> 01:00:13.488
He's the brother to the girl that I used to hate, but I said . . . Used to could swim.

1036
01:00:13.488 --> 01:00:15.591
But me and her are good friends.

1037
01:00:15.591 --> 01:00:17.094
We graduated together and stuff.

1038
01:00:17.094 --> 01:00:18.296
It was her brother.

1039
01:00:18.296 --> 01:00:19.798
What's up there [Lil Breeze?]?

1040
01:00:19.798 --> 01:00:21.000
<v UNKNOWN MALE>Hey! [Lil Cat?]

1041
01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:21.417
<v JACKSON>Alright.

1042
01:00:21.417 --> 01:00:25.170
Anyway, I got hit in it again playing football.

1043
01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:33.092
The doctor told me that I could either have it taken out because I possibly become addicted to medicine,

1044
01:00:33.092 --> 01:00:42.265
I mean drugs, because it was going to be painful all the time, and I would always have to be taking medication.

1045
01:00:42.265 --> 01:00:50.187
So I tells him to go ahead and take it out because I couldn't see out of it anyway.

1046
01:00:50.187 --> 01:00:54.774
So, I had my eye operation, and something I didn't do.

1047
01:00:54.774 --> 01:01:01.028
After I had this operation, they send me to Shreveport, Louisiana to have the operation.

1048
01:01:01.028 --> 01:01:06.866
I had it, and during the operation, I didn't find this out until later,

1049
01:01:06.866 --> 01:01:15.622
but I was to be fitted with a prosthetic and my eye had to heal up for three to four weeks.

1050
01:01:15.622 --> 01:01:18.958
But during the time I was in Shreveport,

1051
01:01:18.958 --> 01:01:26.880
I came back home to Lake Charles and one of . . . to Westlake and to living with my grandmother then.

1052
01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:34.802
So, one day I was looking . . . I was in the bathroom and my eye was itching real . . . started itching.

1053
01:01:34.802 --> 01:01:38.555
This is the eye that they had taken out.

1054
01:01:38.555 --> 01:01:39.806
So it's itching.

1055
01:01:39.806 --> 01:01:44.809
I'm rubbing it and this thing fall out in the [face bowl?].

1056
01:01:44.809 --> 01:01:46.060
I don't know.

1057
01:01:46.060 --> 01:01:48.145
I could see it coming.

1058
01:01:48.145 --> 01:01:49.813
I really tripped out.

1059
01:01:49.813 --> 01:01:51.898
I go, "What is this?

1060
01:01:51.898 --> 01:01:55.233
I can't be growing another eye because [ . . . ?] thing.”

1061
01:01:55.233 --> 01:01:59.820
My eye opening up and it falls into the face bowl.

1062
01:01:59.820 --> 01:02:03.572
It's a little round thing and its hard plastic.

1063
01:02:03.572 --> 01:02:06.491
And, I don't know what this is.

1064
01:02:06.491 --> 01:02:09.410
So, I tell my grandmother what happened.

1065
01:02:09.410 --> 01:02:16.915
She said, "You need to take it to the doctor and he'll probably tell you what it is."

1066
01:02:16.915 --> 01:02:19.000
So I said, "Okay."

1067
01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:22.941
I take it to the doctor and he tells me.

1068
01:02:22.941 --> 01:02:29.640
He said, "They didn't explain this to you, while you were in Shreveport, what they had done?"

1069
01:02:29.640 --> 01:02:35.158
I said, "No." He said, “This was implanted into your eye, into your skin,

1070
01:02:35.158 --> 01:02:43.039
for it to heal up and once it heal the prosthetic is to fit over . . . to fit into your muscle.”

1071
01:02:43.039 --> 01:02:46.980
It was sewn into the muscle behind the back here.

1072
01:02:46.980 --> 01:02:49.739
It was to give my eye movement.

1073
01:02:49.739 --> 01:03:04.320
It was like a round ball and when my eye was like . . . Imagine when it heal up it would be able to move because it would fill out and be . . . The prosthetic would sit right over it.

1074
01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:08.261
Anyway, it came out and I never got it redone.

1075
01:03:08.261 --> 01:03:14.567
That's why my eye . . . I wear this patch because my eye is droopy, like almost closed.

1076
01:03:14.567 --> 01:03:22.448
It’s an artificial eye, but I can have it fixed now with all the medical things and technology of today.

1077
01:03:22.448 --> 01:03:25.207
I'm sure it could be fixed good.

1078
01:03:25.207 --> 01:03:31.118
Anyway, I lost my eye, and I got my arm caught in the washing machine.

1079
01:03:31.118 --> 01:03:33.483
In an old style washing machine.

1080
01:03:33.483 --> 01:03:35.453
My mother told me again.

1081
01:03:35.453 --> 01:03:37.030
See, I was right . . .

1082
01:03:37.030 --> 01:03:38.212
<v ARSENEAULT>Like an open . . .?

1083
01:03:38.212 --> 01:03:39.000
<v JACKSON>Pardon?

1084
01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:40.579
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it like an open bucket?

1085
01:03:40.579 --> 01:03:41.632
How did that work?

1086
01:03:41.632 --> 01:03:42.684
<v JACKSON>Old style washing machine?

1087
01:03:42.684 --> 01:03:42.947
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1088
01:03:42.947 --> 01:03:48.474
<v JACKSON>It was a washing machine but the wringer part . . . the wringer . . . You ever seen a washing machine that have a wringer?

1089
01:03:48.474 --> 01:03:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1090
01:03:49.000 --> 01:03:53.060
<v JACKSON>That goes with two things and you stick the clothes in it and it go through.

1091
01:03:53.060 --> 01:03:53.567
<v ARSENEAULT>Presses it?

1092
01:03:53.567 --> 01:03:54.328
<v JACKSON>It presses it.

1093
01:03:54.328 --> 01:03:55.343
Run the water out.

1094
01:03:55.343 --> 01:03:55.597
Yes.

1095
01:03:55.597 --> 01:03:58.642
Soon as she told me not to do it, turn her back.

1096
01:03:58.642 --> 01:04:03.209
I pick up a towel and try to do like she had been doing and thing caught me.

1097
01:04:03.209 --> 01:04:03.463
Pulling.

1098
01:04:03.463 --> 01:04:03.970
I hollered.

1099
01:04:03.970 --> 01:04:05.239
She turned around and she . . .

1100
01:04:05.239 --> 01:04:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Your hand?

1101
01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:12.031
<v JACKSON>Yes, it had my arm and head and was pulling me in, going up my arm.

1102
01:04:12.031 --> 01:04:13.539
She yanked me out.

1103
01:04:13.539 --> 01:04:14.670
I survived that.

1104
01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:20.325
I got hit by a car, yes, on a Sunday, in front of the church.

1105
01:04:20.325 --> 01:04:23.340
Me and this guy playing . . . He's alive today.

1106
01:04:23.340 --> 01:04:24.848
Lives in Denver, Colorado.

1107
01:04:24.848 --> 01:04:27.110
My brother's married to his sister.

1108
01:04:27.110 --> 01:04:29.749
Anyway, at that time I got hit.

1109
01:04:29.749 --> 01:04:33.518
This old white man hit me, but it wasn't intentional.

1110
01:04:33.518 --> 01:04:37.288
It was my fault because I ran into the street.

1111
01:04:37.288 --> 01:04:44.073
We throwing little shell rocks at each other in front of . . . We was coming out of Sunday school.

1112
01:04:44.073 --> 01:04:45.958
We playing in the road.

1113
01:04:45.958 --> 01:04:53.497
I run into the road the dude. . . He hit the brakes but he don't run over me he hits me,

1114
01:04:53.497 --> 01:04:55.382
but he knocks me down.

1115
01:04:55.382 --> 01:04:58.398
He hit the brakes and slid into me.

1116
01:04:58.398 --> 01:05:01.791
I survived that, but I'm lucky to be here.

1117
01:05:01.791 --> 01:05:06.691
Blessed to be here, because I had a lot of adventures coming up.

1118
01:05:06.691 --> 01:05:07.445
For real.

1119
01:05:07.445 --> 01:05:10.461
All of this happened before I was ten.

1120
01:05:10.461 --> 01:05:14.607
Yes, my mother died when I was ten years old . . . 1957.

1121
01:05:14.607 --> 01:05:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You must have given her a . . . some anxiety.

1122
01:05:18.000 --> 01:05:18.462
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1123
01:05:18.462 --> 01:05:20.308
I was her baby.

1124
01:05:20.308 --> 01:05:20.769
<v ARSENEUALT>Yes.

1125
01:05:20.769 --> 01:05:24.462
Did you have any favorite teachers at Mossville?

1126
01:05:24.462 --> 01:05:25.846
<v JACKSON>Yes, Ms. Rogers.

1127
01:05:25.846 --> 01:05:26.769
Great lady.

1128
01:05:26.769 --> 01:05:27.692
English teacher.

1129
01:05:27.692 --> 01:05:29.538
Everybody liked Ms. Rogers.

1130
01:05:29.538 --> 01:05:31.385
She was a [whip?].

1131
01:05:31.385 --> 01:05:33.231
She's still alive too.

1132
01:05:33.231 --> 01:05:37.846
You ever heard of anybody else talk about Ms. Rogers?

1133
01:05:37.846 --> 01:05:42.000
Somebody else said that was their favorite teacher?

1134
01:05:42.000 --> 01:05:45.030
<v ARSENEAULT>They said it “behooves” you.

1135
01:05:45.030 --> 01:05:47.455
<v JACKSON>Yes, and “it's deplorable.”

1136
01:05:47.455 --> 01:05:53.515
If you made a bad statement, used the incorrect grammar.

1137
01:05:53.515 --> 01:05:56.545
Like, "Jackson that is deplorable."

1138
01:05:56.545 --> 01:05:59.576
Yes, Ms. Rogers was cool.

1139
01:05:59.576 --> 01:06:03.818
Look, this is a funny thing too.

1140
01:06:03.818 --> 01:06:11.697
She told Joe [Marrow?] . . . Joe Marrow was . . . You know how you take pictures?

1141
01:06:11.697 --> 01:06:15.939
Your pictures get sent off then you . . .

1142
01:06:15.939 --> 01:06:17.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Yearbook pictures?

1143
01:06:17.152 --> 01:06:18.364
<v JACKSON>I guess.

1144
01:06:18.364 --> 01:06:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, like a professional picture?

1145
01:06:22.000 --> 01:06:25.830
<v JACKSON>No. Well I guess you could say a yearbook.

1146
01:06:25.830 --> 01:06:31.362
They would let you take pictures and then they'll send pictures to you.

1147
01:06:31.362 --> 01:06:35.191
You could select the pictures you want or whatever.

1148
01:06:35.191 --> 01:06:39.447
Joe Marrow . . . We're in class and the pictures came back.

1149
01:06:39.447 --> 01:06:41.574
And we're in English class.

1150
01:06:41.574 --> 01:06:48.809
Ms. Rogers had the pictures so she's giving everybody pictures, letting them look at them and stuff.

1151
01:06:48.809 --> 01:06:54.766
And Joe Marrow said, "Oh Ms. Rogers, I don't want these old ugly pictures."

1152
01:06:54.766 --> 01:06:56.894
Ms. Rogers say, "Marrow, Joseph.

1153
01:06:56.894 --> 01:07:02.000
The camera took what it saw." [laughs] Oh man we laughed.

1154
01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:03.975
Ms. Rogers was so smooth.

1155
01:07:03.975 --> 01:07:07.531
The . . . Let’s see how she said about the fingers.

1156
01:07:07.531 --> 01:07:09.111
Something about the fingers.

1157
01:07:09.111 --> 01:07:12.272
The moving finger writes, having written moves on.

1158
01:07:12.272 --> 01:07:19.383
She was always . . . Like somebody at the board, “Hey . . .” Ms. Rogers writing at the board, her back turned.

1159
01:07:19.383 --> 01:07:21.753
"Hey man, what time is it?"

1160
01:07:21.753 --> 01:07:24.519
Ms. Rogers turn around, "Time is passing.

1161
01:07:24.519 --> 01:07:27.679
Are you?" [laughs] Oh, she was something else.

1162
01:07:27.679 --> 01:07:28.864
She still alive.

1163
01:07:28.864 --> 01:07:33.605
She got to be close to ninety, but she still look good.

1164
01:07:33.605 --> 01:07:38.741
I saw her maybe about four or five month ago at a funeral.

1165
01:07:38.741 --> 01:07:40.321
I talked to her.

1166
01:07:40.321 --> 01:07:43.481
Yes, she's a . . . Man, she was the lady.

1167
01:07:43.481 --> 01:07:43.877
Yes.

1168
01:07:43.877 --> 01:07:48.617
Ms. Rogers and Ms. Jones were the favorite teachers of most everybody.

1169
01:07:48.617 --> 01:07:51.383
She was a young pretty lady, tall.

1170
01:07:51.383 --> 01:07:53.358
That's my cousin right there.

1171
01:07:53.358 --> 01:08:00.074
He was a drummer used to be in a Grambling's band back in the day [Grambling University].

1172
01:08:00.074 --> 01:08:03.235
When Grambling was at the top of it.

1173
01:08:03.235 --> 01:08:04.420
<v ARSENEAULT>The Tiger band?

1174
01:08:04.420 --> 01:08:04.815
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1175
01:08:04.815 --> 01:08:05.210
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1176
01:08:05.210 --> 01:08:06.000
Coach . . .

1177
01:08:06.000 --> 01:08:07.189
<v JACKSON>He was a drummer.

1178
01:08:07.189 --> 01:08:07.486
Yes.

1179
01:08:07.486 --> 01:08:10.162
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we're at an hour if you're ready to . . .

1180
01:08:10.162 --> 01:08:10.459
<v JACKSON>Huh?

1181
01:08:10.459 --> 01:08:13.135
<v ARSENEAULT>We can wrap it up a little bit here.

1182
01:08:13.135 --> 01:08:17.000
<v JACKSON>Yes if you . . . I can talk as long as you ask questions.

1183
01:08:17.000 --> 01:08:19.400
<v ARSENEAULT>I got a lot of questions.

1184
01:08:19.400 --> 01:08:21.800
<v JACKSON>I'm . . . Oh, you out of questions?

1185
01:08:21.800 --> 01:08:25.400
<v ARSENEAULT>No, I've got a lot is what I'm saying.

1186
01:08:25.400 --> 01:08:25.800
<v JACKSON>Alright.

1187
01:08:25.800 --> 01:08:27.400
<v ARSENEAULT>I got a ton.

1188
01:08:27.400 --> 01:08:31.800
I just don’t want you to miss out on your reunion.

1189
01:08:31.800 --> 01:08:32.200
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1190
01:08:32.200 --> 01:08:33.400
Okay, well . . . Okay.

1191
01:08:33.400 --> 01:08:36.600
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess I can ask you one more.

1192
01:08:36.600 --> 01:08:37.000
<v JACKSON>Okay.

1193
01:08:37.000 --> 01:08:40.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Then we can kind of wrap it up.

1194
01:08:40.200 --> 01:08:43.000
What are your fondest memories of Mossville?

1195
01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:44.200
<v JACKSON>The fondest memory?

1196
01:08:44.200 --> 01:08:46.200
Man, that's hard to say.

1197
01:08:46.200 --> 01:08:49.000
I had a lot of good times.

1198
01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:54.200
Well my fondest memory is what I tell you about the track tournament.

1199
01:08:54.200 --> 01:08:56.200
That was part of Mossville.

1200
01:08:56.200 --> 01:08:58.200
That was my fondest memory.

1201
01:08:58.200 --> 01:09:07.400
The way he ran this guy down and the way the fans from the other school was hollering for him to catch him.

1202
01:09:07.400 --> 01:09:09.800
That would be my fondest memory.

1203
01:09:09.800 --> 01:09:11.800
I always remember that guy.

1204
01:09:11.800 --> 01:09:13.000
He's still alive.

1205
01:09:13.000 --> 01:09:15.000
He lives in Missouri.

1206
01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:17.136
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did you graduate?

1207
01:09:17.136 --> 01:09:17.564
<v JACKSON>1967.

1208
01:09:17.564 --> 01:09:20.555
<v ARSENEAULT>When did they start integrating the schools?

1209
01:09:20.555 --> 01:09:21.409
<v JACKSON>About 1968.

1210
01:09:21.409 --> 01:09:21.836
Yes.

1211
01:09:21.836 --> 01:09:24.827
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that time?

1212
01:09:24.827 --> 01:09:29.955
<v JACKSON>I was out of school and . . . I remember that my friend Brenda,

1213
01:09:29.955 --> 01:09:39.355
the one that I tell you how I was friends with her and her birthday was on the same day as me?

1214
01:09:39.355 --> 01:09:43.200
We was . . . Viola and Brenda was doing the hopscotch.

1215
01:09:43.200 --> 01:09:50.036
I remember her as being the first black person, her and this girl names Mathilda Bates,

1216
01:09:50.036 --> 01:09:53.455
to go to school in Westlake . . . to integrate.

1217
01:09:53.455 --> 01:09:55.591
And it was in 1968.

1218
01:09:55.591 --> 01:09:58.582
I think she was a senior then.

1219
01:09:58.582 --> 01:09:59.436
But Yes.

1220
01:09:59.436 --> 01:10:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did the community react?

1221
01:10:02.000 --> 01:10:06.109
<v JACKSON>Well, it wasn't any big protest . . . not openly, or whatever.

1222
01:10:06.109 --> 01:10:11.040
We always had some people who had feeling about things like that.

1223
01:10:11.040 --> 01:10:20.079
But it wasn't anything that was a violent protest or anything like that . . . or even a protest where the community came out.

1224
01:10:20.079 --> 01:10:21.312
A peaceful protest.

1225
01:10:21.312 --> 01:10:23.366
It wasn't open like that.

1226
01:10:23.366 --> 01:10:29.119
Just people accepted it, whether they liked it or not, but they accepted it.

1227
01:10:29.119 --> 01:10:31.995
It wasn't anything where anybody got hurt.

1228
01:10:31.995 --> 01:10:37.748
But another thing that happened in Westlake, but it was concerning Mossville people too,

1229
01:10:37.748 --> 01:10:43.911
because the guy had gone to school here when he was to become the mayor.

1230
01:10:43.911 --> 01:10:46.787
I know you heard about that, right?

1231
01:10:46.787 --> 01:10:48.431
When somebody killed him.

1232
01:10:48.431 --> 01:10:51.307
Well we all say somebody killed him.

1233
01:10:51.307 --> 01:10:52.540
They called it . . .

1234
01:10:52.540 --> 01:10:55.005
<v ARSENEAULT>The day after he was elected?

1235
01:10:55.005 --> 01:10:59.114
<v JACKSON>The day before he was going to be sworn in.

1236
01:10:59.114 --> 01:11:01.168
He had already been elected.

1237
01:11:01.168 --> 01:11:06.921
The vote had come, but he was supposed to be sworn in that Monday.

1238
01:11:06.921 --> 01:11:09.386
Somebody killed him at the school.

1239
01:11:09.386 --> 01:11:15.550
They say he committed suicide, but I know better than that, and everybody else does.

1240
01:11:15.550 --> 01:11:17.193
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

1241
01:11:17.193 --> 01:11:19.248
<v JACKSON>Probably about five years ago.

1242
01:11:19.248 --> 01:11:20.891
<v ARSENEAULT>That was in Westlake?

1243
01:11:20.891 --> 01:11:22.946
<v JACKSON>It was here in Mossville.

1244
01:11:22.946 --> 01:11:24.178
At the school.

1245
01:11:24.178 --> 01:11:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Here?

1246
01:11:25.000 --> 01:11:26.600
<v JACKSON>In Mossville at the school just down here.

1247
01:11:26.600 --> 01:11:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1248
01:11:27.000 --> 01:11:27.400
<v JACKSON>In the . . .

1249
01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In the school?

1250
01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:29.200
<v JACKSON>Found him in the parking lot.

1251
01:11:29.200 --> 01:11:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, they found him in the parking lot.

1252
01:11:31.000 --> 01:11:31.353
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1253
01:11:31.353 --> 01:11:34.176
Shot in the chest with a three-fifty-seven magnum.

1254
01:11:34.176 --> 01:11:37.000
And this is the hope to God truth.

1255
01:11:37.000 --> 01:11:42.647
I was living in Houston and I just happened to be home for the New Year.

1256
01:11:42.647 --> 01:11:47.588
My brother had come from California and whatever and we were down in Westlake,

1257
01:11:47.588 --> 01:11:52.529
and this guy I asked you about, Owen [Kayhee?] that you talked to him.

1258
01:11:52.529 --> 01:11:53.588
He called me.

1259
01:11:53.588 --> 01:11:57.118
We're good friends, and he calls me that same night.

1260
01:11:57.118 --> 01:12:04.529
Invites me here in Mossville to come and eat some gumbo and play cards with some people that I didn't know.

1261
01:12:04.529 --> 01:12:09.118
And I tell him, I said, "I don't know those people or anything.

1262
01:12:09.118 --> 01:12:15.118
He said, "Well I wouldn't have called you and told you to come if it wasn't cool."

1263
01:12:15.118 --> 01:12:16.529
So I said, "Okay.

1264
01:12:16.529 --> 01:12:19.000
If you say it's cool, it's cool."

1265
01:12:19.000 --> 01:12:22.176
And he was inviting me to come down here.

1266
01:12:22.176 --> 01:12:25.000
We in Westlake, me and my wife.

1267
01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:32.758
So we leave Westlake to come to Mossville, and as we pass the school, there's the police tape and stuff,

1268
01:12:32.758 --> 01:12:38.964
there's a fire engine, a couple of police cars, and a truck in the parking lot.

1269
01:12:38.964 --> 01:12:42.455
And I tell my wife, I say, "Something happened.

1270
01:12:42.455 --> 01:12:45.945
Maybe somebody was doing drugs around here or something.”

1271
01:12:45.945 --> 01:12:47.885
But somebody got killed here.

1272
01:12:47.885 --> 01:12:49.824
So we go on down.

1273
01:12:49.824 --> 01:12:51.764
We go to the place.

1274
01:12:51.764 --> 01:13:00.685
To this house. and we had waited about fifteen minutes and somebody calls and said that . . . Now this . . . The guy who got killed,

1275
01:13:00.685 --> 01:13:13.097
his nickname was [Teddy?] and they had this other guy from Sulphur [Louisiana] who had been to this place where me and my wife went to . . . where Owen had invited us to.

1276
01:13:13.097 --> 01:13:15.812
His name is [Tookie?] . . . [Tooty and Tookie?].

1277
01:13:15.812 --> 01:13:22.406
So when we get there somebody called and Tookie is already gone but I didn't see him.

1278
01:13:22.406 --> 01:13:25.121
When I got there he was gone.

1279
01:13:25.121 --> 01:13:29.000
But he had just left, according to what happened.

1280
01:13:29.000 --> 01:13:35.154
Somebody called on the phone and the house lady pick up and she talking and say,

1281
01:13:35.154 --> 01:13:39.385
"Oh no, that can't be true, he just left from here."

1282
01:13:39.385 --> 01:13:42.077
And she hangs up. [?] saying, "Who's calling?

1283
01:13:42.077 --> 01:13:42.846
What's up?"

1284
01:13:42.846 --> 01:13:50.923
And she say, "Somebody killed [Tookie?] at the school, and they, "Ah no." It can’t be true, he just left here.

1285
01:13:50.923 --> 01:13:54.000
What would he be doing at the school?”

1286
01:13:54.000 --> 01:13:57.846
Somebody suggested, "Call his house and see if he there."

1287
01:13:57.846 --> 01:14:00.154
He was staying with his mother.

1288
01:14:00.154 --> 01:14:07.462
They had the number so the lady called, the house lady called and said, "Ms. Jones, is [Tookie?] home?"

1289
01:14:07.462 --> 01:14:10.923
And the lady from what she said, "He is?"

1290
01:14:10.923 --> 01:14:14.000
She say . . . The house lady say, "You sure?"

1291
01:14:14.000 --> 01:14:15.538
She said, “I’m sure."

1292
01:14:15.538 --> 01:14:17.077
Because she said, "Okay.

1293
01:14:17.077 --> 01:14:18.231
Okay, thank you."

1294
01:14:18.231 --> 01:14:19.385
She hands up.

1295
01:14:19.385 --> 01:14:20.154
What happened?

1296
01:14:20.154 --> 01:14:22.077
He home in the bed.

1297
01:14:22.077 --> 01:14:24.769
His mother said he in the bed.

1298
01:14:24.769 --> 01:14:28.231
So we say, "Somebody just probably playing a joke."

1299
01:14:28.231 --> 01:14:30.154
That's not cool or whatever.

1300
01:14:30.154 --> 01:14:34.000
We hang up and started to play cards again.

1301
01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:37.117
About less than two minutes the phone rings again.

1302
01:14:37.117 --> 01:14:38.849
She one the phone, "What?

1303
01:14:38.849 --> 01:14:41.274
Oh no. No, that can't be true."

1304
01:14:41.274 --> 01:14:43.006
The lady busts out crying.

1305
01:14:43.006 --> 01:14:44.737
Her husband, "What's wrong honey?

1306
01:14:44.737 --> 01:14:45.430
What's wrong?"

1307
01:14:45.430 --> 01:14:46.816
"It's [Tootie?], the mayor.

1308
01:14:46.816 --> 01:14:47.855
Somebody killed him."

1309
01:14:47.855 --> 01:14:50.626
He was found dead in the school yard.

1310
01:14:50.626 --> 01:14:53.743
And me and my wife had passed by everything.

1311
01:14:53.743 --> 01:15:01.017
So I said, "That must have been what was happening when we passed because . . ." And look, but we go down there.

1312
01:15:01.017 --> 01:15:04.480
And we say, "Let's go back and see what happened."

1313
01:15:04.480 --> 01:15:12.447
So we go back, me and my wife, this time when we get there the schoolyard is full of people from the community.

1314
01:15:12.447 --> 01:15:13.832
Both police is there.

1315
01:15:13.832 --> 01:15:14.872
Lot of police.

1316
01:15:14.872 --> 01:15:16.257
They have a tape.

1317
01:15:16.257 --> 01:15:19.374
We come in the school yard into the school.

1318
01:15:19.374 --> 01:15:24.570
This is in the parking lot by the gym where the body is and stuff.

1319
01:15:24.570 --> 01:15:26.994
The body is laying on the ground.

1320
01:15:26.994 --> 01:15:29.419
They have not covered it up yet.

1321
01:15:29.419 --> 01:15:30.458
They should have.

1322
01:15:30.458 --> 01:15:33.229
Let me make sure if it was covered.

1323
01:15:33.229 --> 01:15:36.000
Yes, it was covered at the time.

1324
01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:45.912
But when we come in the driveway and get out the car and we walk down in front of the school going toward the parking lot,

1325
01:15:45.912 --> 01:15:51.630
we come up toward the tape . . . where one part of the tape is we stop.

1326
01:15:51.630 --> 01:15:56.968
But we are maybe about forty yards from the body lying on the ground.

1327
01:15:56.968 --> 01:15:59.636
People are walking all around him there.

1328
01:15:59.636 --> 01:16:01.543
I mean, like nobody's stopping.

1329
01:16:01.543 --> 01:16:05.355
You're not supposed . . . That's not supposed to happen like that.

1330
01:16:05.355 --> 01:16:07.261
People are walking all around.

1331
01:16:07.261 --> 01:16:09.548
The police standing up and stuff.

1332
01:16:09.548 --> 01:16:15.648
While we're there and stuff they pick the body take the car and take the car,

1333
01:16:15.648 --> 01:16:29.372
put it on a truck on the . . . one of these trucks like where you have a wreck and you can tilt the truck back and pull it all in there and put his truck on there.

1334
01:16:29.372 --> 01:16:36.616
They wind up taking the truck to Westlake, back to the forensics place where they can look at it.

1335
01:16:36.616 --> 01:16:38.903
They take it to his house.

1336
01:16:38.903 --> 01:16:41.953
Washed the blood and all of this down.

1337
01:16:41.953 --> 01:16:43.859
Wash it off the ground.

1338
01:16:43.859 --> 01:16:45.003
Moved the body.

1339
01:16:45.003 --> 01:16:47.672
Washed it down and all of this.

1340
01:16:47.672 --> 01:16:49.959
None . . . This is bad police work.

1341
01:16:49.959 --> 01:16:53.390
I know all of this not supposed to happen.

1342
01:16:53.390 --> 01:16:59.109
Especially the people walking all around with a crime scene . . . inside the tape and stuff.

1343
01:16:59.109 --> 01:17:03.302
Anyway, this all happens between ten and twelve o'clock at night.

1344
01:17:03.302 --> 01:17:06.352
The morning paper comes out about five o'clock.

1345
01:17:06.352 --> 01:17:08.639
I'm back at my mother-in-law house.

1346
01:17:08.639 --> 01:17:10.164
I get the paper.

1347
01:17:10.164 --> 01:17:12.452
I'm looking for my lottery number.

1348
01:17:12.452 --> 01:17:17.408
When I look in the paper mayor . . . The newly elected mayor commits suicide.

1349
01:17:17.408 --> 01:17:22.364
How can you come to that conclusion in a few hours like that?

1350
01:17:22.364 --> 01:17:30.370
That's . . . I have a lot of the papers right now about the [fuel?] and they even exhumed his body and stuff.

1351
01:17:30.370 --> 01:17:34.563
Looked at it again and sent his body to Philadelphia [Pennsylvania].

1352
01:17:34.563 --> 01:17:40.282
People raised money and stuff and helped the family do another autopsy and all that.

1353
01:17:40.282 --> 01:17:43.713
And it come up with an inconclusive thing still.

1354
01:17:43.713 --> 01:17:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you think happened?

1355
01:17:46.000 --> 01:17:48.486
<v JACKSON>That somebody killed him, a white person.

1356
01:17:48.486 --> 01:17:54.879
One of those old haters, because a lot of the young white people helped to get him elected.

1357
01:17:54.879 --> 01:18:00.206
Because it's more white people in Westlake than blacks and he was a well-liked guy.

1358
01:18:00.206 --> 01:18:01.981
He was kind of young.

1359
01:18:01.981 --> 01:18:03.757
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a black man?

1360
01:18:03.757 --> 01:18:04.112
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1361
01:18:04.112 --> 01:18:05.533
He's a black guy.

1362
01:18:05.533 --> 01:18:06.243
<v ARSENEAULT>From Mossville?

1363
01:18:06.243 --> 01:18:07.308
<v JACKSON>Yes, from Westlake.

1364
01:18:07.308 --> 01:18:08.019
<v ARSENEAULT>From Westlake?

1365
01:18:08.019 --> 01:18:10.505
<v JACKSON>But he went to school here too.

1366
01:18:10.505 --> 01:18:15.477
This had been before integration came that he went to school here in Mossville.

1367
01:18:15.477 --> 01:18:19.028
But he had been an alderman . . . alderman you call it?

1368
01:18:19.028 --> 01:18:20.093
It's an alderman.

1369
01:18:20.093 --> 01:18:23.290
It's somebody like one the city council or something.

1370
01:18:23.290 --> 01:18:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1371
01:18:24.000 --> 01:18:24.782
<v JACKSON>An alderman.

1372
01:18:24.782 --> 01:18:25.173
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1373
01:18:25.173 --> 01:18:27.519
<v JACKSON>I think they call it that.

1374
01:18:27.519 --> 01:18:29.083
Well he was that.

1375
01:18:29.083 --> 01:18:37.686
And then anyway, the guy who he had defeated was an old white man who had been the mayor for twenty-four years.

1376
01:18:37.686 --> 01:18:47.071
And a lot of people believe in . . . Well you if you're going to be real about it a lot of people don't like change.

1377
01:18:47.071 --> 01:18:50.199
They want it to stay the status quo.

1378
01:18:50.199 --> 01:18:52.936
Wanted to stay just like this forever.

1379
01:18:52.936 --> 01:18:54.500
That’s . . . Somebody killed him.

1380
01:18:54.500 --> 01:18:56.064
That' what I believe.

1381
01:18:56.064 --> 01:18:59.192
That's what the majority of black people believe.

1382
01:18:59.192 --> 01:19:06.622
How can you reach a conclusion that quick from between ten and twelve, and wash all the evidence down?

1383
01:19:06.622 --> 01:19:11.314
And take the . . . What'd you take the truck to his house for?

1384
01:19:11.314 --> 01:19:13.269
Who ever heard of that?

1385
01:19:13.269 --> 01:19:17.179
And who took it to his house was a policeman.

1386
01:19:17.179 --> 01:19:17.962
<v ARSENEAULT>From Westlake?

1387
01:19:17.962 --> 01:19:18.353
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1388
01:19:18.353 --> 01:19:19.135
From Westlake.

1389
01:19:19.135 --> 01:19:21.090
<v ARSENEAULT>Working for the old mayor.

1390
01:19:21.090 --> 01:19:21.481
<v JACKSON>Huh?

1391
01:19:21.481 --> 01:19:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Probably working . . . Maybe working for the old major.

1392
01:19:25.000 --> 01:19:25.318
<v JACKSON>Maybe.

1393
01:19:25.318 --> 01:19:25.637
Whatever.

1394
01:19:25.637 --> 01:19:27.866
They took the truck to his house.

1395
01:19:27.866 --> 01:19:32.325
I don't know if they ever did a forensic type thing on the truck,

1396
01:19:32.325 --> 01:19:36.783
do what the police are supposed to do, or the crime lab or whatever.

1397
01:19:36.783 --> 01:19:38.694
But yes, and that actually happened.

1398
01:19:38.694 --> 01:19:44.745
I was here and I saw this with my own eyes when they washed the blood down and all.

1399
01:19:44.745 --> 01:19:50.159
With a lot of the people from the community . . . At first it was just a few people,

1400
01:19:50.159 --> 01:19:53.025
like a couple of police cars and all that.

1401
01:19:53.025 --> 01:19:56.529
Had a fire truck was there early when we first passed.

1402
01:19:56.529 --> 01:20:05.127
But between the time we went down to where they were having the gumbo and playing cards and by the time we went back to the scene,

1403
01:20:05.127 --> 01:20:13.408
well couldn't have been more than forty minutes, and they had a lot of people there by that time we got down there the second time.

1404
01:20:13.408 --> 01:20:17.568
But yes, they had . . . I know another thing that happened.

1405
01:20:17.568 --> 01:20:20.992
A friend of ours got killed . . . got hung.

1406
01:20:20.992 --> 01:20:23.561
Think some white people did this.

1407
01:20:23.561 --> 01:20:27.413
Some white . . . young men called the [Yan?] Brothers. [ . . .?] Mayfield.

1408
01:20:27.413 --> 01:20:31.693
It's a guy . . . Seven of us would always be together.

1409
01:20:31.693 --> 01:20:39.398
It was me and my two brothers, the three Mayfield boys, it was two of them were twins.

1410
01:20:39.398 --> 01:20:42.822
Then they had another brother called Paul Tracey.

1411
01:20:42.822 --> 01:20:46.674
And then there neighbor Papa Joe, his name is [ . . .?].

1412
01:20:46.674 --> 01:20:48.386
Everybody still living though.

1413
01:20:48.386 --> 01:20:50.955
One of my brothers is dead.

1414
01:20:50.955 --> 01:20:56.091
The one that shot me with the bow and arrow, he's dead.

1415
01:20:56.091 --> 01:20:59.943
And Calvin, that's the one that was found hung.

1416
01:20:59.943 --> 01:21:02.939
And the rest of us still living.

1417
01:21:02.939 --> 01:21:05.508
But I was ten years old.

1418
01:21:05.508 --> 01:21:14.496
That was the same year my mother died and my mother died in December of . . . The twenty-second of December in 1957.

1419
01:21:14.496 --> 01:21:23.057
In November of 1957 Calvin Mayfield was thirteen years old, and he was found hung in a tree in Westlake.

1420
01:21:23.057 --> 01:21:25.197
They call that a suicide.

1421
01:21:25.197 --> 01:21:32.045
Hung with a brand new rope with a hangman's knot, and said that he committed suicide.

1422
01:21:32.045 --> 01:21:35.470
But in those days white people was cold.

1423
01:21:35.470 --> 01:21:48.311
They call you “nigger” like it was nothing and passed by you at night hit you with a baseball bat and lean out the car throw piss at you, anything.

1424
01:21:48.311 --> 01:21:57.727
And you couldn't . . . Best not go to the police you might get put in jail yourself if you go make a complaint.

1425
01:21:57.727 --> 01:21:59.439
On the real, yes.

1426
01:21:59.439 --> 01:22:03.292
They could do anything and get away with it.

1427
01:22:03.292 --> 01:22:08.000
And they came to the conclusion it was a suicide.

1428
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:15.854
What happened, like his mother we were in the area where he was found was a place we had a tree called the African tree.

1429
01:22:15.854 --> 01:22:22.137
And the tree was real large and it had large branches and you can jump up on . . . muscle up on.

1430
01:22:22.137 --> 01:22:27.478
We’re kids we could get up on there and walk under the limbs and all of this.

1431
01:22:27.478 --> 01:22:29.363
So they were looking for him.

1432
01:22:29.363 --> 01:22:31.248
We had been in the woods.

1433
01:22:31.248 --> 01:22:34.075
We were playing in the woods digging up holes.

1434
01:22:34.075 --> 01:22:36.903
Make a little for like people come through there.

1435
01:22:36.903 --> 01:22:39.416
Cover up with leaves and step in it.

1436
01:22:39.416 --> 01:22:41.301
We're real little boys growing up.

1437
01:22:41.301 --> 01:22:42.243
Mischief and stuff.

1438
01:22:42.243 --> 01:22:44.757
Hiding and watch somebody step in the hole.

1439
01:22:44.757 --> 01:22:49.783
Laugh and take off running and stuff, making bird traps and all this kind of thing.

1440
01:22:49.783 --> 01:22:53.239
We did a lot of things like that in the woods.

1441
01:22:53.239 --> 01:22:56.381
We’d make us a bird trap and thing or whatever.

1442
01:22:56.381 --> 01:22:59.836
But we was in the woods a lot . . . all the time.

1443
01:22:59.836 --> 01:23:03.920
And those [Yan?] Brothers used to come through with some older white guys.

1444
01:23:03.920 --> 01:23:06.434
He was probably nineteen or twenty years old.

1445
01:23:06.434 --> 01:23:08.633
He was, "Niggers get out of here."

1446
01:23:08.633 --> 01:23:10.518
We’d take off running from him.

1447
01:23:10.518 --> 01:23:12.403
We were bigger boys and stuff.

1448
01:23:12.403 --> 01:23:13.973
We just laugh and stuff.

1449
01:23:13.973 --> 01:23:19.000
We had set a thing and one time one of them stepped in a hole.

1450
01:23:19.000 --> 01:23:22.502
Anyway, that's when we leave . . . But this would happen.

1451
01:23:22.502 --> 01:23:23.670
He was missing.

1452
01:23:23.670 --> 01:23:27.562
And we had been in the woods playing and stuff.

1453
01:23:27.562 --> 01:23:29.507
So it's on a Sunday.

1454
01:23:29.507 --> 01:23:35.345
His mother came to our house and Paul Tracey that was not his twin brother.

1455
01:23:35.345 --> 01:23:37.291
His twin was named Charles.

1456
01:23:37.291 --> 01:23:39.626
One was Calvin, one was Charles.

1457
01:23:39.626 --> 01:23:40.793
Calvin was missing.

1458
01:23:40.793 --> 01:23:44.685
She came and . . . [Yes, as mama recalled . . . His other mama.

1459
01:23:44.685 --> 01:23:50.911
Do you have mama do?] "Yes we saw him earlier he said he was going home."

1460
01:23:50.911 --> 01:23:56.360
She said, "Yes he did come home, and I was fixing dinner for him.

1461
01:23:56.360 --> 01:24:00.640
It was about around two, we was going to have dinner.

1462
01:24:00.640 --> 01:24:09.591
His other brothers was home when we were about to eat he wasn't there, and he wasn't next door at Papa Joe's house.

1463
01:24:09.591 --> 01:24:13.094
We wanted to know if he came by here."

1464
01:24:13.094 --> 01:24:15.818
We said, "No. He didn't come here."

1465
01:24:15.818 --> 01:24:18.931
So that was maybe, I guess, around four.

1466
01:24:18.931 --> 01:24:21.655
Something like that in the day time.

1467
01:24:21.655 --> 01:24:25.936
She came back earlier that night about around six . . . six thirty.

1468
01:24:25.936 --> 01:24:27.493
It was dark then.

1469
01:24:27.493 --> 01:24:30.217
"No he still hasn't been . . ." What's up?

1470
01:24:30.217 --> 01:24:31.773
I'm having an interview.

1471
01:24:31.773 --> 01:24:32.163
<v KIM MCKEE>Okay.

1472
01:24:32.163 --> 01:24:34.498
They want to take a picture [ . . .?].

1473
01:24:34.498 --> 01:24:36.054
<v ARSENEAULT>We can stop it.

1474
01:24:36.054 --> 01:24:38.000
<v MCKEE>Can you stop it?

1475
01:24:38.000 --> 01:24:39.739
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes we can stop it.

1476
01:24:39.739 --> 01:24:42.522
I'll . . . We can do a follow up later.

1477
01:24:42.522 --> 01:24:44.609
<v MCKEE>Yes, she can follow up later.

1478
01:24:44.609 --> 01:24:47.043
We going to take a picture darling.

1479
01:24:47.043 --> 01:24:47.391
<v JACKSON>Okay.

1480
01:24:47.391 --> 01:24:49.478
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you for your time.

1481
01:24:49.478 --> 01:24:49.826
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1482
01:24:49.826 --> 01:24:53.304
<v ARSENEAULT>We'll send you a copy and we'll be in touch.

1483
01:24:53.304 --> 01:24:54.000
<v JACKSON>Anyway . . .

1484
01:24:54.000 --> 01:24:56.195
<v ARSENEAULT>Want to finish the story and then we can . . .

1485
01:24:56.195 --> 01:24:56.439
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1486
01:24:56.439 --> 01:24:57.415
<v ARSENEAULT>We can pause it.

1487
01:24:57.415 --> 01:25:00.341
<v JACKSON>Tell them I'm going to be in there in about two minutes.

1488
01:25:00.341 --> 01:25:02.293
<v MCKEE>No, come take your picture and come back.

1489
01:25:02.293 --> 01:25:03.024
They can wait.

1490
01:25:03.024 --> 01:25:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ll pause it.

1491
01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:04.812
<v JACKSON>Okay Yes.

1492
01:25:04.812 --> 01:25:09.681
With [Mama Dew?] and Paul Tracey are . . . the one with the twin.

1493
01:25:09.681 --> 01:25:14.957
They came for the last time around twelve at night to our house.

1494
01:25:14.957 --> 01:25:21.043
Still his mother was real upset, crying and things and she couldn't find her son.

1495
01:25:21.043 --> 01:25:23.072
So night turn into day.

1496
01:25:23.072 --> 01:25:32.812
We went to school that morning about . . . School had taken in at maybe about eight fifteen or so it came over the loud speaker.

1497
01:25:32.812 --> 01:25:34.841
I can't remember right now.

1498
01:25:34.841 --> 01:25:37.681
Yes, the principal's name was Mr. Williams.

1499
01:25:37.681 --> 01:25:44.174
He came over the loud speaker and said that . . . Everybody was aware that Mayfield was missing.

1500
01:25:44.174 --> 01:25:46.203
The students had been told.

1501
01:25:46.203 --> 01:25:51.884
Who did know . . . But they . . . When we went to school everybody knew about it.

1502
01:25:51.884 --> 01:26:07.304
So he came over the speaker and said that Calvin Mayfield had been found and he just told us straight out that he was found hanging in the woods right off of Samson Street and he was dead.

1503
01:26:07.304 --> 01:26:11.768
The girls and some of the guys were crying and things.

1504
01:26:11.768 --> 01:26:19.884
I was one of the guys that was crying because he was a good friend of mine and my brothers.

1505
01:26:19.884 --> 01:26:26.377
After that evening he had been found . . . And during that time it was November and cold.

1506
01:26:26.377 --> 01:26:28.812
Not freezing, but it was cold.

1507
01:26:28.812 --> 01:26:38.551
He was found in what we call the African tree and he was found by his brother, Paul Tracey, and his neighbor Papa Joe.

1508
01:26:38.551 --> 01:26:41.391
He wasn't found by his twin brother.

1509
01:26:41.391 --> 01:26:45.043
But his youngest brother and Papa Joe found him.

1510
01:26:45.043 --> 01:26:56.000
They decided to look through the woods and stuff and they had gone by the African tree and he was found hanging in the African Tree.

1511
01:26:56.000 --> 01:26:59.276
And the way he was positioned . . . Excuse me.

1512
01:26:59.276 --> 01:27:06.647
When I saw the picture in the newspaper it was with a sheet around him . . . around his shoulders,

1513
01:27:06.647 --> 01:27:09.104
and the sheet touched the ground.

1514
01:27:09.104 --> 01:27:16.884
He was in a kneeling position with one knee touching the ground and his other leg kind of bent.

1515
01:27:16.884 --> 01:27:24.255
But one of his knees was touching the ground and you could see the rope hanging . . . I mean,

1516
01:27:24.255 --> 01:27:29.988
the rope coming from the tree around, going down where the sheet covered it.

1517
01:27:29.988 --> 01:27:37.359
You couldn't see around his neck but you could see by the rope that was still on him.

1518
01:27:37.359 --> 01:27:44.320
And when his brother and them found him it was more dramatic because he was just hanging,

1519
01:27:44.320 --> 01:27:52.101
but this was I guess after the police got there or whatever and covered up and stop a picture.

1520
01:27:52.101 --> 01:27:56.196
But they found him in the raw hanging like that.

1521
01:27:56.196 --> 01:28:04.386
And they had like a small investigation and this older white man who ran a store his name was [Rue?].

1522
01:28:04.386 --> 01:28:06.843
He was a mean white man.

1523
01:28:06.843 --> 01:28:12.166
When I say that I'm talking about he was mean to black people.

1524
01:28:12.166 --> 01:28:21.994
But everybody dealt with him because he had a store and you could get credit and a lot of people got credit from him.

1525
01:28:21.994 --> 01:28:25.680
A lot of black families got credit from him.

1526
01:28:25.680 --> 01:28:26.908
He knew everybody.

1527
01:28:26.908 --> 01:28:28.955
He knew all the families.

1528
01:28:28.955 --> 01:28:31.412
I mean, all the black families.

1529
01:28:31.412 --> 01:28:42.059
Because you could send your kids to the store and all they had to do was sign their name and he really knew this Mayfield son,

1530
01:28:42.059 --> 01:28:44.516
or this Jackson's son, or whatever.

1531
01:28:44.516 --> 01:28:47.383
Anyway, he was . . . And he sold rope.

1532
01:28:47.383 --> 01:28:54.754
He was asked because Calvin was found hanging with a brand new rope and with a hangman's knot.

1533
01:28:54.754 --> 01:29:05.810
And old man Rue sold rope and he was asked did Mayfield come in there and buy some rope at any time within the last few days.

1534
01:29:05.810 --> 01:29:09.496
Old man Rue said no that he didn't come.

1535
01:29:09.496 --> 01:29:14.000
None of the Mayfields came in there and bought rope.

1536
01:29:14.000 --> 01:29:18.851
So it was like it wasn't Calvin's rope according to old man.

1537
01:29:18.851 --> 01:29:26.532
I know he was going to tell . . . If he sold some rope he was going to say he did.

1538
01:29:26.532 --> 01:29:30.574
And he said no he didn't sell Mayfield no rope.

1539
01:29:30.574 --> 01:29:35.426
So, therefore he wasn’t hung with a rope that Calvin bought himself.

1540
01:29:35.426 --> 01:29:39.872
Somebody had a brand new rope and hung him with it.

1541
01:29:39.872 --> 01:29:42.298
And that's how it went down.

1542
01:29:42.298 --> 01:29:46.340
It never was anybody found guilty of doing anything wrong.

1543
01:29:46.340 --> 01:29:49.170
It was like Calvin Mayfield committed suicide.

1544
01:29:49.170 --> 01:30:02.106
Probably playing to see . . . This is what it said . . . Probably playing to see what it might have felt like to be hung but not intentionally going all the way through with it.

1545
01:30:02.106 --> 01:30:04.128
Kind of a [mistake?] there.

1546
01:30:04.128 --> 01:30:05.340
That was it.

1547
01:30:05.340 --> 01:30:13.830
Yes, like he was testing it out to see what it might be like and over did it in other words.

1548
01:30:13.830 --> 01:30:15.851
That’s how that went down.

1549
01:30:15.851 --> 01:30:18.277
That was in 1957 in Westlake.

1550
01:30:18.277 --> 01:30:21.915
And everything I'm telling you is real and true.

1551
01:30:21.915 --> 01:30:28.383
You can believe it all happened, everything that happened to me to all these other incidents.

1552
01:30:28.383 --> 01:30:28.787
Yes.

1553
01:30:28.787 --> 01:30:30.000
Go ahead.

1554
01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:30.418
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

1555
01:30:30.418 --> 01:30:31.255
Last question.

1556
01:30:31.255 --> 01:30:34.600
I'll ask you a wrap-up question and then . . .

1557
01:30:34.600 --> 01:30:35.018
<v JACKSON>Okay.

1558
01:30:35.018 --> 01:30:36.691
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . we can be done.

1559
01:30:36.691 --> 01:30:39.200
What does Mossville mean to you?

1560
01:30:39.200 --> 01:30:42.127
<v JACKSON>Oh, it means a lot of memories.

1561
01:30:42.127 --> 01:30:47.564
A lot of great memories that lay with me until I pass on.

1562
01:30:47.564 --> 01:30:51.327
That's about the best that I can describe it,

1563
01:30:51.327 --> 01:30:59.691
because you really can’t describe everything that happened and all the feeling that you experience over the years and things,

1564
01:30:59.691 --> 01:31:07.218
but it's been a great community to grow up in and a great community to come back to.

1565
01:31:07.218 --> 01:31:13.909
Like I'm here today for this little probably. . . the last part . . . next to the last party.

1566
01:31:13.909 --> 01:31:24.782
The last gathering among the people of Mossville per say because it’s supposed to be buying all the land and everything in here in this area.

1567
01:31:24.782 --> 01:31:27.709
So this probably is the last hurrah.

1568
01:31:27.709 --> 01:31:31.055
But it means a great deal to me.

1569
01:31:31.055 --> 01:31:32.727
It means everything really.

1570
01:31:32.727 --> 01:31:35.236
It's a whole way of life.

1571
01:31:35.236 --> 01:31:36.491
It's going down.

1572
01:31:36.491 --> 01:31:36.909
Yes.

1573
01:31:36.909 --> 01:31:39.000
So there it is.

1574
01:31:39.000 --> 01:31:42.721
<v ARSENEAULT>Is there anything else you want to share for the recording?

1575
01:31:42.721 --> 01:31:44.074
<v JACKSON>No. That's about it.

1576
01:31:44.074 --> 01:31:44.412
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1577
01:31:44.412 --> 01:31:44.750
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1578
01:31:44.750 --> 01:31:45.088
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

1579
01:31:45.088 --> 01:31:47.794
Well thank you so much for your time.

1580
01:31:47.794 --> 01:31:51.176
We appreciate it and we'll be sending you a copy.

1581
01:31:51.176 --> 01:31:51.515
<v JACKSON>Okay.

1582
01:31:51.515 --> 01:31:53.544
You find me an interesting interview?

1583
01:31:53.544 --> 01:31:53.882
<v ARSENEAULT>Huh?

1584
01:31:53.882 --> 01:31:56.926
<v JACKSON>I said, do you find me an interesting interview?

1585
01:31:56.926 --> 01:31:57.603
<v ARSENEAULT>You're okay.

1586
01:31:57.603 --> 01:31:58.279
You're okay!

1587
01:31:58.279 --> 01:31:58.618
<v JACKSON>Yes.

1588
01:31:58.618 --> 01:31:58.956
Okay.

1589
01:31:58.956 --> 01:32:02.000
I see you did a lot of laughing.

1590
01:32:02.000 --> 01:32:02.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4535.

1591
01:32:02.000 --> 01:32:02.200
End Session I.]
